-k\\).*  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).*  \n   In this case we have\n   \\[\n   a\\le n+1-b\\le k .\n   \\]\n   Define \\(t = b-a-(n-k)\\).  \n   Because \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a\\le k\\), we obtain\n   \\[\n   1\\le t\\le k .\n   \\]\n   Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   b = a + (n-k+t),\n   \\]\n   i.e. it lies on the sunny line \\(S_{t}\\).\n\n   Thus every lattice point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n4. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   The construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\), each of which has slope \\(1\\) and therefore is sunny. All remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny. Therefore the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all such integers are admissible, and no other integer can occur because the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, which is \\(n\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]"
