d  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t\\cap S =\\{(a,\\,t a-(t-1))\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,n+1-t\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) equals \\(t\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_t\\) then \\(b=t a-(t-1)\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(a+t a-(t-1)\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n+1-t\\).  \nConversely, for any integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\) the point \\((a, t a-(t-1))\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge1\\), \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); thus it belongs to \\(S\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\).  \nTake the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) from Lemma 2.  \nDelete from \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal lines \\(y=0, y=1,\\dots ,y=k-1\\) (the line \\(y=0\\) is not in \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) but is removed for bookkeeping).  \nNow add the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=2,\\;x=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=k+1\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k .\n\\]\n\nDenote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \nIt contains\n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\);\n* \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=k+1\\);\n* \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=k+(k)+(n-k)=n\\), and all lines are distinct because the vertical and horizontal families have mutually different equations, and each \\(\\ell_t\\) has a slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Covering property of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\))\n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S\\).\n\n*Case 1.*  There exists \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) such that \\(b=t a-(t-1)\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_t\\subset\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Case 2.*  No such \\(t\\) exists.  \