 of the line through the two points equals \\(-1\\).  \nThus \\(L\\) would be parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(L\\) is sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\), Lemma\u202f1 implies that any collection of sunny lines can contribute at most one point to each diagonal. Consequently the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by sunny lines is at most \\(n\\).\n\nSince  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\nat least \\(|T_n|-n\\ge n-2\\) points must be covered by non\u2011sunny lines.  \nEach non\u2011sunny line can cover arbitrarily many points (it may be horizontal, vertical, or have slope \\(-1\\)), but we have only \\(n\\) lines in total. Therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe first exhibit a configuration with **no** sunny lines.\n\n#### Baseline configuration ( \\(k=0\\) )\nTake the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\; y=b\\qquad (b=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=n .\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\). Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, and none of the lines is sunny. Hence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nNow fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSelect distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nb_1,b_2,\\dots ,b_k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y=-\\frac12x+\\Bigl(b_j+\\frac12\\Bigr). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n