\nSubstituting \\((a,b)\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\nb=-\\frac12 a+b_j+\\frac12\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a+2b=2b_j+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is (3). Conversely, any integer solution of (3) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies (2), so it lies on \\(S_j\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nEquation (3) defines a “skew’’ diagonal distinct for each \\(j\\) because the right‑hand side \\(2b_j+1\\) is different for distinct \\(b_j\\). Hence the sets  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\cap S_j\\;(j=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Remaining lines\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(b\\in\\mathcal B\\) keep the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (as in the baseline).  \nTogether with the vertical line \\(V\\) we have exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal B|+(k)+1 = (n-1-k)+k+1 = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{H_b\\mid b\\in\\mathcal B\\}\\cup\\{V\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\in\\mathcal B\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n* If \\(b=b_j\\) for some \\(j\\) then \\(a+2b= a+2b_j\\).  \n  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b_j\\le n-1\\), we have \\(a\\le n+1-b_j\\le n\\).  \n  Moreover \\(a\\) is a positive integer, so \\(a+2b_j\\) is an odd integer between \\(3\\) and \\(2n-1\\).  \n  Since the right‑hand side of (3) for the line \\(S_j\\) equals \\(2b_j+1\\), the equality \\(a+2b=2b_j+1\\) holds exactly when \\(a=1\\).  \n  In that case \\((1,b_j)\\) belongs to \\(S_j\\) by (2).  \n  If \\(a\\neq1\\) then \\(a+2b\\neq2b_j+1\\); however such a point must have \\(b\\le n-1\\) and therefore belongs to the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\) (which is present because \\(b\\notin\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_k\\}\\) only when \\(b\\) is not one of the selected indices).  \n\n* The only point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\); it lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause the construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, we have exhibited a configuration for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines existed.  \nThen there would be exactly one non‑sunny line, say \\(L\\).  \n\n*If \\(L\\) is horizontal or vertical*, it can intersect at most one diagonal \\(D_s\\) in more than one point; all other diagonals would have to be covered solely by sunny lines, each contributing at most one point per diagonal (Lemma 1). Hence at least one diagonal would remain uncovered – contradiction.\n\n*If \\(L\\) has slope \\(-1\\)*, it meets each diagonal in exactly one point, so the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines could contribute at most one additional point per diagonal, giving at most \\(2n\\) covered points. Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>2n\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), the covering condition fails.\n\nThus \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.  \n\nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, Lemma 1 implies that each diagonal contributes at most one point, so at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) could be covered, contradicting \\(|T_n|>\\!n\\). Hence \\(k=n\\) is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe constructions of Section 2 show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \nThe impossibility arguments of Section 3 rule out larger values of \\(k\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
