[\n   S_t:\\; y = x + (h+1+t) .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The slope of each \\(S_t\\) is \\(1\\); therefore none of them is parallel to the horizontal, vertical or diagonal directions, i.e. all \\(S_t\\) are sunny and they are pairwise distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\n   We now verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Case 1: \\(b=1\\).*  \n   The point lies on \\(L_1\\).\n\n   *Case 2: \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\neq 1\\).*  \n   The point lies on \\(L_2\\).\n\n   *Case 3: \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\).*  \n   Then \\(a+b\\le n+1 = h+k+2\\).  \n   If \\(a+b = h+2\\) the point lies on the diagonal line \\(L_3\\).  \n   Otherwise \\(a+b\\ge h+3\\). Set\n   \\[\n   t:=a+b-(h+2) .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a+b\\le h+k+2\\) we have \\(1\\le t\\le k\\). Moreover\n   \\[\n   b = (a+b)-(a) = (h+2+t)-a = a + (h+1+t) .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(b-a = h+1+t\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies equation (3) for this value of \\(t\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_t\\).\n\n   All possibilities are covered, so the \\(n\\) constructed lines satisfy the covering condition, and exactly \\(k\\) of them (the \\(S_t\\)) are sunny.\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines existed.  \n   As observed above, at most three of the \\(n\\) lines can be non\u2011sunny, so at least \\(k-(n-3)\\ge 1\\) sunny lines are pairwise non\u2011parallel. Each such line meets each of the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) in at most one point, giving at most \\(3\\bigl(k-(n-3)\\bigr)\\) incidences between these sunny lines and the points of \\(T_n\\). Adding the at most three incidences contributed by the possible non\u2011sunny lines we obtain the same inequality as in (2), namely \\(k\\le n-2\\), which contradicts \\(k\\ge n-1\\). Hence configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) cannot exist.\n\nCombining the upper bound (2) with the explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"
