n\\[\nP_j=(j,\\;n+1-j),\\qquad \nM_j=\\text{the line through }(0,0)\\text{ and }P_j .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(M_j\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_j=\\frac{n+1-j}{j}>0 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m_j\\neq0\\) (numerator \\(>0\\)), \\(m_j\\neq\\infty\\) (denominator \\(>0\\)), and \\(m_j\\neq-1\\) (both numerator and denominator are positive), each \\(M_j\\) is sunny.\n\nThe function \\(j\\mapsto m_j\\) is strictly decreasing on \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently the slopes \\(m_1,\\dots ,m_k\\) are pairwise distinct, so the lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) are distinct and none of them is vertical.\n\n*Non‑sunny (vertical) lines.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct vertical lines, each parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore not sunny.\n\n#### 3.2 Covering \\(S_n\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\ge k+1\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(L_a\\) is among our set, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it because its abscissa is \\(a\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nFrom the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nSince also \\(b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a\\ge 1\\), the only point of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) is \\((a,n+1-a)=P_a\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)=P_a\\), which lies on the sunny line \\(M_a\\).\n\nHence every element of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n#### 3.3 Counting sunny lines  \n\nExactly the lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\) are sunny, so the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(S_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with the obvious upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) from (2), this shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dot