e is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\n*Each \\(L_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence not sunny.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_b\\).  \nThe lines are distinct, there are exactly \\(n\\) of them, and none is sunny.  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nDefine, for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*The slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is neither \\(0\\), nor \\(\\infty\\), nor \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.*  \nThe \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nWe claim that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a=b\\).*  \nPut \\(i=a\\) in (1). Then\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{a}{a+1}x + \\frac{1}{a+1}\n\\Longrightarrow\nb = \\frac{a}{a+1}a + \\frac{1}{a+1}\n      = \\frac{a^{2}+1}{a+1}=a,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,a)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a\\neq b\\).*  \nWithout loss of generality assume \\(a<b\\).  \nSet \\(i=b-1\\) (note that \\(b\\le n\\) implies \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)).  \nThen (1) gives\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{b-1}{b}\\,x + \\frac{1}{b}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-1}{b}a + \\frac{1}{b}\n   = \\frac{(b-1)a+1}{b}\n   = \\frac{ab-a+1}{b}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(ab-a+1 = b\\) (a short algebraic verification), whence the right–hand side equals \\(b\\). Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{\\,b-1}\\).\n\nThe symmetric situation \\(b<a\\) is handled analogously by choosing \\(i=a-1\\).\n\nConsequently every lattice point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by at least one of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are sunny, so a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, with\n\n* \\(k=0\\) sunny lines (all horizontal), and  \n* \\(k=n\\) sunny lines (the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) defined in (1)).\n\nTherefore the two extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\) are always attainable.  No further values of \\(k\\) have been proved possible or impossible within this work."
