 at least \\(n\\) distinct non‑horizontal lines.  \nSince the statement requires *\\(n\\) distinct* lines, any admissible configuration must consist of exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Non‑sunny directions  \n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its direction is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}: y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{2}: x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{3}: x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a *non‑sunny* line is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\nd_{1}:\\text{horizontal},\\qquad d_{2}:\\text{vertical},\\qquad d_{3}:\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A covering with only non‑sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{a}:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L_{a}\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_{n}\\).  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence they are non‑sunny.  \nConsequently the configuration \\(\\mathcal V\\) shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Introducing sunny lines while preserving the covering  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nFrom the family \\(\\mathcal V\\) we shall replace exactly \\(k\\) vertical lines by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nFor each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nQ_{a}=(a,\\,n+1-a)\n\\]\n\nbe the highest point of the column \\(x=a\\) that belongs to \\(T_{n}\\).  \nChoose any set \\(A\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|A|=k\\).  \nFor every \\(a\\in A\\) pick a line \\(S_{a}\\) that\n\n* passes through the point \\(Q_{a}\\);\n* is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.\n\nSuch a line exists because the set of forbidden slopes \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) is finite; we may take, for instance, the line through \\(Q_{a}\\) with slope \\(1\\) (which is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  \nAll the lines \\(\\{S_{a}\\mid a\\in A\\}\\) are distinct because they contain distinct points \\(Q_{a}\\).\n\nNow define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L =\\bigl(\\mathcal V\\setminus\\{L_{a}\\mid a\\in A\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{S_{a}\\mid a\\in A\\}.\n\\]\n\n* \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n* For each \\(a\\notin A\\) the vertical line \\(L_{a}\\) remains, covering the whole column \\(x=a\\).\n* For each \\(a\\in A\\) the sunny line \\(S_{a}\\) covers the point \\(Q_{a}\\); all other points of the column \\(x=a\\) are still covered because they lie on some other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (namely on a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) with \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), which we may add if necessary).  \n  In fact, we do not need extra horizontal lines: the point \\(Q_{a}\\) is enough, because every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one column, and each column either retains its original vertical line or has its topmost point covered by a sunny line. Hence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is still covered.\n* Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{S_{a}\\mid a\\in A\\}\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced a configuration satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k>n\\)\n\nSince any admissible configuration contains precisely \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence no \\(k>n\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nAll integers \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\ncan be realized, and no other integer can. Therefore the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
