lace a suitable subset of the sunny lines by non\u2011sunny vertical lines, while preserving the covering property.\n\n   *Construction for a given \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ).*  \n   - Keep the first \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \n   - For each \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) replace the sunny line \\(L_j\\) by the vertical line  \n     \\[\n     V_j:\\;x=j .\n     \\]\n\n   The set of lines is therefore  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{L}_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   **Why the covering still holds.**  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then \\(a=j\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_j\\).  \n   - If \\(a\\le k\\) then, by the Covering Lemma, \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). Because the index \\(i\\) is given by \\(i=\\dfrac{na}{a+1-b}\\) and \\(a\\le k\\), we have \\(i\\le k\\); hence the line is one of the retained sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\).\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\) with \\(i\\le k\\)) and \\(n-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines, all distinct. Hence it realises the prescribed value of \\(k\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nA line is either sunny or not; with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines we can have at most \\(n\\) sunny ones. Hence any feasible \