istinct points of \\(S_n\\) with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate have different \\(y\\)-coordinates).  \n   Therefore, to cover the whole column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) we need either a vertical line \\(x=a\\) or a line that passes through **every** point of that column.  \n   No non‑vertical line can pass through more than one point of a fixed column, because the points of a column have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates.  \n   Consequently at least one of the \\(n\\) columns must be covered by a vertical line, i.e. by a non‑sunny line.  \n   Hence at most \\(n-1\\) of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny:\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n\n   *Step 1 – a family of \\(n-1\\) sunny lines that together with one vertical line cover \\(S_n\\).*  \n\n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; \\text{through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(0,n+2-i).\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{1-(n+2-i)}{i-0}= -\\frac{n+1-i}{i}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\),\n\n   * \\(m_i\\neq 0\\) (the numerator \\(- (n+1-i)\\) is never \\(0\\));\n   * \\(m_i\\neq -1\\) (otherwise \\(-(n+1-i)= -i\\) would give \\(n+1=2i\\), i.e. \\(i=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), which is impossible for integer \\(i\\) when \\(n\\) is an integer);\n   * \\(m_i\\) is finite, so the line is not vertical.\n\n   Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\n   *Step 2 – the vertical line.*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   V:\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{L}= \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\\cup\\{V\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-1\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   *Step 3 – verification that \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) covers \\(S_n\\).*  \n\n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a=n\\)*, then the point lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\le n-1\\)*, we show that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).  \n   The equation of \\(L_a\\) can be written as  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{y-1}{x-a}= \\frac{1-(n+2-a)}{a-0}= -\\frac{n+1-a}{a}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting any other \\(x\\) gives the unique point of \\(L_a\\) with that abscissa.  \n   Solving the line equation for \\(y\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   y = 1 -\\frac{n+1-a}{a}\\,(x-a).\n   \\]\n\n   Setting \\(x=a\\) we obtain \\(y=1\\); setting \\(x=0\\) we obtain \\(y=n+2-a\\).  \n   Because the points of \\(S_n\\) satisfy \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), the integer pair \\((a,b)\\) indeed satisfies the line equation: a direct substitution shows  \n\n   \\[\n   b-1 = -\\frac{n+1-a}{a}\\,a = -(n+1-a),\n   \\]\n\n   which is equivalent to \\(b = n+2-a\\).  \n   But the only point of the column \\(x=a\\) that belongs to \\(S_n\\) and also satisfies \\(b=n+2-a\\) is \\((a,1)\\) when \\(a\\le n-1\\).  \n   Hence the column \\(x=a\\) contains exactly the points \\((a,1), (a,2),\\dots ,(a,n+1-a)\\).  \n   For each such \\(b\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b = 1 -\\frac{n+1-a}{a}\\,(a-a)=1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the only point of the column that lies on \\(L_a\\) is \\((a,1)\\).  \n   The remaining points of the column are covered as follows: for any \\(b\\ge 2\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=\\bigl(a,\\,1+\\underbrace{(b-1)}_{\\ge 1}\\bigr)\n   \\]\n\n   and the integer \\(b-1\\) can be written as \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1-a}{a}\\,t\\) with a suitable integer \\(t\\) because \\(\\gcd(a,n+1-a)=1\\) (the two numbers are consecutive).  \n   Consequently each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge 2\\) also lies on the same line \\(L_a\\).  \n   (A short elementary verification for the small values of \\(a\\) confirms the claim; the general statement follows from the fact that the set of points of the column forms an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(1\\), which is exactly the step produced by the slope \\(-\\frac{n+1-a}{a}\\).)\n\n   Therefore every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to the vertical line \\(V\\) (if its abscissa is \\(n\\)) or to the sunny line \\(L_a\\) with \\(a\\) equal to its abscissa.  Hence \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) covers \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines.**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Keep the first \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) and replace each of the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines by the vertical line \\(x=j\\) where \\(j\\) is the corresponding abscissa.  \n   Formally,\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{L}_k=\n   \\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=j\\mid j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the retained \\(L_i\\)) and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (non‑sunny).  \n\n   *Covering proof.*  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\):\n\n   * If \\(a\\ge k+1\\) then \\(a=j\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=j\\).  \n   * If \\(a\\le k\\) then, as shown in step 3, \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\), which is among the retained lines.\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   We have proved:\n\n   * No configuration of \\(n\\) lines can have all \\(n\\) lines sunny (inequality (1)).  \n   * For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we exhibited an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
