ite slope. A vertical line is not sunny. ∎  \n\n   Because the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) each contain at least two points, covering all points of a column without a vertical line would require at least two distinct non‑vertical lines, each contributing at most one point of that column (Lemma 1). Hence for every column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) we **must** use a vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is not sunny) in order not to exceed the total of \\(n\\) lines.  \n\n   The only column that does not force a vertical line is \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\). Consequently at most one of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, giving  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   They are all non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(S_n\\) (each point lies on the line whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals its first coordinate). Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Existence for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   (non‑sunny) together with **one** sunny line that passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Choose any slope \\(m\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\neq 0,\\;m\\neq -1,\\;m\\neq \\infty .\n   \\]\n\n   For concreteness we may take \\(m=1\\). The line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y-1 = 1\\,(x-n) \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad y = x-n+1\n   \\]\n\n   is sunny (its slope is \\(1\\)) and contains the point \\((n,1)\\). All other points of \\(S_n\\) lie on one of the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\). Thus the family of \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{L\\}\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   By (1) no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines, while the constructions in §§ 3–4 show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are achievable. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0