            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the errors in the previous construction and now present a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) one can arrange \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) so that exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny **iff** \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) the *column*  \n   \\[\n   C_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of the points \\((a,1),(a,2),\\dots ,(a,n+1-a)\\).  \n   The columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) contain at least two points; \\(C_n\\) contains the single point \\((n,1)\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le 1\\).**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line (i.e. a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\)) meets each column \\(C_a\\) in **at most one** point of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Proof.* If a line is not vertical it has a well‑defined slope \\(m\\). Two distinct points of the same column have the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate but different \\(y\\)‑coordinates, so they cannot lie on a line of finite slope. A vertical line is not sunny. ∎  \n\n   Because the columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_{n-1}\\) each contain at least two points, covering all points of a column without a vertical line would require at least two distinct non‑vertical lines, each contributing at most one point of that column (Lemma 1). Hence for every column \\(C_a\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) we **must** use a vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is not sunny) in order not to exceed the total of \\(n\\) lines.  \n\n   The only column that does not force a vertical line is \\(C_n=\\{(n,1)\\}\\). Consequently at most one of the \\(n\\) lines can be sunny, giving  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   They are all non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(S_n\\) (each point lies on the line whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals its first coordinate). Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Existence for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   (non‑sunny) together with **one** sunny line that passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Choose any slope \\(m\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\neq 0,\\;m\\neq -1,\\;m\\neq \\infty .\n   \\]\n\n   For concreteness we may take \\(m=1\\). The line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y-1 = 1\\,(x-n) \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad y = x-n+1\n   \\]\n\n   is sunny (its slope is \\(1\\)) and contains the point \\((n,1)\\). All other points of \\(S_n\\) lie on one of the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\). Thus the family of \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{L\\}\\) covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   By (1) no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines, while the constructions in §§ 3–4 show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are achievable. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0