; the only way to contain two points with the same \\(x\\)‑coordinate is to be vertical. ∎\n\n4. **Counting argument giving the upper bound \\(k\\le 1\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Denote  \n\n   * \\(v\\) – the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n   * \\(k\\) – the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n   * the remaining \\(n-v-k\\) lines are non‑vertical and non‑sunny (i.e. horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n   **(i) Columns with at least two points.**  \n   For each \\(x\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the column \\(C_x\\) contains at least two points.  \n   By Lemma 2 a non‑vertical line can contribute **at most one** of these points.  \n   Consequently, to obtain the two points of \\(C_x\\) we need either  \n\n   * a vertical line \\(x=\\text{const}\\) (which supplies *both* points), or  \n   * *two* distinct non‑vertical lines, each providing one of the points.\n\n   **(ii) Minimal number of non‑vertical lines.**  \n   Suppose we have \\(v\\) vertical lines. Then the columns that are *not* covered by a vertical line each require **two** non‑vertical lines.  \n   There are \\(n-1\\) columns in total, so the number of columns lacking a vertical line is at most \\(n-1-v\\).  \n   Hence the total number of non‑vertical lines needed is at least  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\,(n-1-v).\n   \\]\n\n   Because the whole family contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   v + 2\\,(n-1-v)\\le n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\ge n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus at least \\(n-2\\) of the lines are vertical.\n\n   **(iii) Consequence for sunny lines.**  \n   The total number of non‑vertical lines equals \\(n-v\\).  \n   From (2),\n\n   \\[\n   n-v\\le n-(n-2)=2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence there are at most two non‑vertical lines altogether.  \n   If both of them were sunny, we would have \\(k=2\\).  \n   But two distinct sunny lines have different slopes, and by Lemma 1 each of them can meet a given column in at most one point.  \n   Therefore the two sunny lines together can supply at most one point to each of the \\(n-1\\) columns that need two points, leaving at least one column uncovered – a contradiction.\n\n   Consequently **at most one** of the non‑vertical lines can be sunny, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Realisation of the bounds.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); all lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with a single sunny line that passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   For instance, the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = x-n+1\n   \\]\n\n   has slope \\(1\\) (different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)) and contains \\((n,1)\\).  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{L\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(S_n\\), and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (3) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines, while the constructions above demonstrate that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are achievable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
