            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\nC_x=\\{(x,b)\\in S_n\\}\\;(1\\le x\\le n).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\) we have \\(|C_x|=n+1-x\\ge 2\\); for \\(x=n\\) we have \\(|C_n|=1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical, then \\(\\ell\\) meets each column \\(C_x\\) in at most one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct points of the same column have the same abscissa. A non‑vertical line has a unique finite slope, so it cannot contain two points with the same abscissa. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.**  \nLet \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) be two distinct non‑vertical lines. Their intersection has abscissa  \n\n\\[\nx_0=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{m_1-m_2},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_i\\) is written \\(y=m_i x+b_i\\).  \nIf each of \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) contains a point of every column \\(C_x\\) for \\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\), then \\(x_0\\) is an integer satisfying \\(1\\le x_0\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.* Solving the two linear equations gives the formula for \\(x_0\\).  \nFor each integer \\(x\\) the numbers \\(y_i=m_i x+b_i\\) are integers (they are the \\(y\\)-coordinates of points of \\(S_n\\)). Hence the difference  \n\n\\[\n(m_1-m_2)x+(b_1-b_2)\n\\]\n\nis an integer for every integer \\(x\\). Varying \\(x\\) by \\(1\\) shows that \\(m_1-m_2\\) itself is an integer; denote it by \\(d\\neq0\\). Then \\(b_1-b_2\\) is also an integer, and\n\n\\[\nx_0=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{m_1-m_2}= -\\frac{b_1-b_2}{d}\\in\\mathbb Q\n\\]\n\nhas denominator dividing \\(|d|\\). Because both lines meet the first column \\(C_1\\), we have \\(x_0\\ge 1\\); because they meet the last column \\(C_{n-1}\\), we have \\(x_0\\le n-1\\). Since \\(x_0\\) is rational with denominator dividing \\(|d|\\) and lies between two consecutive integers, it must be an integer. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S_n\\).  \nWrite  \n\n* \\(v\\) – the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* \\(k\\) – the number of sunny lines (slope \\(\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\));  \n* the remaining \\(n-v-k\\) lines are non‑vertical but not sunny, i.e. they are horizontal (\\(m=0\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\).\n\nBy Lemma 1, any non‑vertical line contributes at most one point to each column.  \nFor a column \\(C_x\\) with \\(|C_x|\\ge2\\) (\\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\)) we therefore need either\n\n* a vertical line \\(x=\\text{const}\\) (which supplies both points of the column), or  \n* two distinct non‑vertical lines, each supplying one point.\n\nHence, if \\(v\\) vertical lines are present, the remaining \\(n-1-v\\) columns each require at least two non‑vertical lines. Consequently\n\n\\[\nn-v\\;\\ge\\;2\\,(n-1-v)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad v\\ge n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\nn-v\\le 2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus at most two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non‑vertical.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge2\\). Then both non‑vertical lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny (otherwise we would have a non‑sunny non‑vertical line, increasing the number of non‑vertical lines beyond the bound (2)).  \n\nBy Lemma 2 the two sunny lines intersect at a point whose abscissa \\(x_0\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le x_0\\le n-1\\). In column \\(C_{x_0}\\) the two lines give the same point, i.e. only one point of that column is covered. Since \\(|C_{x_0}|\\ge2\\), the second point of this column must be supplied by a vertical line, which would raise the number of non‑vertical lines to at least three, contradicting (2).  \n\nTherefore \\(k\\le1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\). Every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); none of the lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n**Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y = x-n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(1\\) (different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)) and passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\). The family \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{L\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(S_n\\), and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince \\(k\\le1\\) for any admissible configuration and both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\), the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
