they are horizontal (\\(m=0\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\).\n\n   By Lemma 1, any non‑vertical line contributes at most one point to each column.  \n\n   For a column \\(C_x\\) with \\(|C_x|\\ge 2\\) (that is, \\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\)) we must cover **both** points of the column.  \n   There are two possibilities:\n\n   * a vertical line \\(x=\\text{const}\\) (which supplies both points of the column), or  \n   * two distinct non‑vertical lines, each supplying one point.\n\n   No column needs more than two lines, because each column contains exactly two points when \\(x\\le n-1\\).  \n   Therefore, after the \\(v\\) vertical lines have taken care of the columns they occupy, the remaining \\(n-1-v\\) columns each require **at least** two non‑vertical lines. Hence\n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{(n-v)}_{\\text{total non‑vertical lines}}\n   \\;\\ge\\;\n   2\\,(n-1-v) .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\ge n-2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n-v\\le 2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus **at most two** lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non‑vertical.\n\n5. **Bounding the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n   Because of (4) there are at most two non‑vertical lines, so both of them must be sunny (otherwise we would have a non‑sunny non‑vertical line and would need a third non‑vertical line to cover the columns).  \n\n   Apply Lemma 2 to these two sunny lines. Their intersection abscissa \\(x_0\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le x_0\\le n-1\\). In column \\(C_{x_0}\\) the two lines therefore give the **same** lattice point, i.e. only one point of that column is covered. Since \\(|C_{x_0}|\\ge 2\\), the second point of this column must be supplied by a vertical line. That would introduce a third non‑vertical line, contradicting (4).  \n\n   Hence the assumption \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible and we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Realisation of the bounds.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); none of the lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = x-n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L\\) has slope \\(1\\) (different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)) and passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{L\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(S_n\\), and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (5) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines, while the explicit constructions above demonstrate that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are achievable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
