_1-m_2\\,}\n   \\]\n\n   is an integer satisfying \\(1\\le x_0\\le n-1\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   For each integer \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\) the numbers  \n\n   \\[\n   y_i(x)=m_i x+b_i\\;(i=1,2)\n   \\]\n\n   are integers, because they are the \\(y\\)-coordinates of points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Hence for every such \\(x\\)\n\n   \\[\n   (m_1-m_2)x+(b_1-b_2)=y_1(x)-y_2(x)\\in\\mathbb Z .\\tag{★}\n   \\]\n\n   Taking the difference of (★) for two consecutive values \\(x\\) and \\(x+1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (m_1-m_2)\\in\\mathbb Z .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=1\\) into (★) and using (1) shows that \\(b_1-b_2\\) is also an integer.  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   x_0=\\frac{b_2-b_1}{m_1-m_2}\n   \\]\n\n   is a rational number whose denominator divides the integer \\(|m_1-m_2|\\); therefore \\(x_0\\) is rational with denominator \\(1\\) or a divisor of \\(|m_1-m_2|\\).\n\n   Because each line meets the first column \\(C_1\\), we have a point \\((1,y_i(1))\\) on each line, so the intersection point \\((x_0,y_0)\\) must lie on the segment joining the two points of \\(C_1\\). The abscissae of those two points are both \\(1\\); the only way two distinct non‑vertical lines through \\((1,y_1(1))\\) and \\((1,y_2(1))\\) can intersect is at an \\(x\\)-coordinate strictly between the smallest and largest \\(x\\)-coordinates of the points they contain. Since every column \\(C_x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\) contains a point of each line, the intersection cannot lie to the left of column 1 nor to the right of column \\(n-1\\); otherwise one of the lines would miss a required point. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x_0\\le n-1 .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(x_0\\) is not an integer. Write \\(x_0=p/q\\) in lowest terms with \\(q>1\\). From (1) we know \\(m_1-m_2\\) is an integer, so the denominator \\(q\\) must divide \\(|m_1-m_2|\\). Choose an integer \\(x\\equiv p\\pmod q\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\) (possible because the interval contains at least \\(q\\) consecutive integers). For this \\(x\\) the left‑hand side of (★) equals  \n\n   \\[\n   (m_1-m_2)x+(b_1-b_2)= (m_1-m_2)\\frac{p}{q}+ (b_1-b_2) \\notin\\mathbb Z,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\((m_1-m_2)p/q\\) has denominator \\(q\\) while \\(b_1-b_2\\) is an integer. This contradicts (★). Hence \\(q=1\\) and \\(x_0\\) is an integer. Together with (2) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   x_0\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n   \\qquad\\blacksquare\n   \\]\n\n4. **Counting non‑vertical lines (corrected).**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Denote  \n\n   * \\(v\\) – the number of vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n   * \\(k\\) – the number of *sunny* lines (slope \\(\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\));  \n   * the remaining \\(n-v-k\\) lines are non‑vertical but not sunny, i.e. they are horizontal (\\(m=0\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\).\n\n   By Lemma 1 each non‑vertical line supplies **at most one** point to each column.  \n   For a column \\(C_x\\) with \\(|C_x|=2\\) (that is, \\(1\\le x\\le n-1\\)) we must cover both points. There are only two ways to do this:\n\n   * a vertical line \\(x=\\text{const}\\) (which supplies both points of the column), or  \n   * two distinct non‑vertical lines, each supplying one of the two points.\n\n   No column needs more than two lines, because a non‑vertical line cannot give two points of the same column.  \n\n   Let \\(V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of abscissae of the vertical lines; \\(|V|=v\\). Those columns are already completely covered. For each of the remaining \\(n-1-v\\) columns we therefore need **at least two** non‑vertical lines. Consequently the total number of non‑vertical lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   n-v\\;\\ge\\;2\\,(n-1-v).\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\ge n-2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n-v\\le 2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **at most two** lines of the family are non‑vertical.\n\n5. **Bounding the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n   By (4) there are at most two non‑vertical lines, so both of them must be sunny (otherwise we would have a non‑sunny non‑vertical line and would need a third non‑vertical line to cover the columns).  \n\n   Apply Lemma 2 to these two sunny lines. Their intersection abscissa \\(x_0\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le x_0\\le n-1\\). In column \\(C_{x_0}\\) the two lines therefore meet the same lattice point, i.e. they together cover **only one** of the two points of that column. The second point of \\(C_{x_0}\\) must be supplied by a vertical line, which would be a third non‑vertical line, contradicting (4).  \n\n   Hence the assumption \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible and we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Realisation of the bounds.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); none of the lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = x-n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L\\) has slope \\(1\\) (different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)) and passes through the remaining point \\((n,1)\\).  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{L\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(S_n\\), and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (5) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines, while the explicit constructions above demonstrate that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are achievable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task 