\n\n   is a rational number whose denominator divides the integer \\(|m_1-m_2|\\); therefore \\(x_0\\) is an integer.\n\n   Both lines meet column \\(C_1\\); if \\(x_0<1\\) the two lines would intersect to the left of that column and could not both pass through a point of \\(C_1\\). Similarly, if \\(x_0>n-1\\) they would intersect to the right of column \\(C_{n-1}\\) and could not both meet a point of that column. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le x_0\\le n-1 .\n   \\qquad\\blacksquare\n   \\]\n\n4. **Counting non\u2011vertical lines.**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Denote  \n\n   * \\(v\\) \u2013 the number of **vertical** lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n   * \\(k\\) \u2013 the number of **sunny** lines (slope \\(\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\));  \n   * the remaining \\(n-v-k\\) lines are non\u2011vertical but not sunny, i.e. they are either horizontal (\\(m=0\\)) or have slope \\(-1\\).\n\n   By Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011vertical line contributes at most one point to each column.  \n   Consider a column \\(C_x\\) that is **not** covered by a vertical line.  \n   Since \\(|C_x|=n+1-x\\ge 2\\) for every \\(x\\le n-1\\), at least \\(|C_x|\\) distinct non\u2011vertical lines are needed to supply the points of that column.  \n   Consequently, if a column \\(C_x\\) is not vertical, we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{number of non\u2011vertical lines}\\;\\ge\\;|C_x| .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(V\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of abscissae of the vertical lines; \\(|V|=v\\).  \n   Among the remaining columns \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus V\\) the **largest** size is attained at the smallest index not in \\(V\\).  \n   Denote this index by \\(x_{\\min}\\). Then  \n\n   \\[\n   |C_{x_{\\min}}| = n+1-x_{\\min}\\ge n-v .\n   \\]\n\n   Applying (2) to the column \\(C_{x_{\\min}}\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   n-v\\;\\le\\;\\text{number of non\u2011vertical lines}=n-v .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the inequality is actually an equality: the family must contain exactly \\(n-v\\) non\u2011vertical lines, and **all** of them are needed to cover the points of the column \\(C_{x_{\\min}}\\).\n\n   In particular, the number of non\u2011vertical lines cannot exceed \\(2\\); otherwise the smallest uncovered column would have size at least \\(3\\) and would require at least three distinct non\u2011vertical lines, contradicting the fact that we have only \\(n-v\\) of them. Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   n-v\\le 2 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Bounding the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n   By (3) there are at most two non\u2011vertical lines, so both of them must be sunny (otherwise we would have a non\u2011sunny non\u2011vertical line and would need a third non\u2011vertical line to satisfy (3)).  \n\n   Apply Lemma\u202f2 to these two sunny lines. Their intersection abscissa \\(x_0\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le x_0\\le n-1\\). In column \\(C_{x_0}\\) the two lines meet the **same** lattice point, i.e. together they cover only one of the \\(|C_{x_0}|\\ge 2\\) points of that column. The remaining point(s) of \\(C_{x_0}\\) must be supplied by a third line. That third line cannot be vertical (otherwise column \\(C_{x_0}\\) would be completely covered, contradicting the fact that the two sunny lines already meet a point of that column). Hence the third line would be a non\u2011vertical, non\u2011sunny line, which would raise the total number of non\u2011vertical lines to at least three, contradicting (3).  \n\n   Therefore \\(k\\) cannot be \\(2\\) or larger, and we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Realisation of the bounds.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); none of the lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = x-n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L\\) has slope \\(1\\) (different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)) and passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) that is not covered by the vertical lines.  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{L\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(S_n\\), and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (4) shows that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines, while the explicit constructions above demonstrate that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are achievable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]"
