min}}|=2\\) and each line supplies a different point.  \n   Thus we must have \\(n-v=2\\) and consequently \\(v=n-2\\).\n\n   Now look at the next column to the right, \\(C_{x_{\\min}+1}\\). Its size is  \n\n   \\[\n   |C_{x_{\\min}+1}| = n - x_{\\min} = (n-v) + (v - x_{\\min}) = 2 + (v - x_{\\min})\\ge 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(x_{\\min}+1\\) is not a vertical column (otherwise it would be the smallest non‑vertical column), the two sunny lines can again contribute at most two points to \\(C_{x_{\\min}+1}\\).  \n   The remaining point(s) of this column would have to be supplied by a third non‑vertical line, contradicting the bound \\(n-v=2\\).  \n\n   Therefore the assumption \\(k\\ge 2\\) leads to a contradiction, and we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\le 1}.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Realisation of the bounds.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\); none of the lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   together with the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y = x-n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L\\) has slope \\(1\\) (different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)) and passes through the unique point \\((n,1)\\) that is not covered by the vertical lines.  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{L\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(S_n\\), and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality \\(k\\le 1\\) shows that no configuration can have two or more sunny lines, while the explicit constructions above demonstrate that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are achievable for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###