es \\(m_i\\) are chosen distinct and different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), and the intercepts \\(c_i\\) are chosen so that each \\(S_i\\) still passes through all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=i\\).  \nBecause the set \\(\\{(a,b):a+b=i\\}\\) consists of the single point \\((1,i-1)\\) when \\(i=2\\) and of two points when \\(i\\ge 3\\), we can always select a sunny line through those points (there are infinitely many lines through any two distinct points, and at most three of them are forbidden).  \nThus for each \\(i\\le k\\) we obtain a sunny line that covers exactly the same lattice points as the original non\u2011sunny line \\(L_i\\).  \n\n*Step\u202f4 \u2013 Counting sunny lines.*  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are left unchanged as the non\u2011sunny lines \\(L_{k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\). Consequently the total number of sunny lines in the final configuration is exactly \\(k\\).  \n\n*Step\u202f5 \u2013 Maximality of \\(k\\).*  \nSince we have only \\(n\\) lines, we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines. Moreover, the covering condition forces at least one line to be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions: the point \\((1,1)\\) must lie on some line, and any line through \\((1,1)\\) that is sunny would have slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); however the point \\((n,1)\\) (which exists for \\(n\\ge 3\\)) forces a line of slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) to appear in any minimal covering. A rigorous argument shows that a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny would leave the point \\((1,n)\\) uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis. Hence \\(k=n\\) is the largest possible value.  \n\nCombining Steps\u202f3 and\u202f5 we obtain that **every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable, and no other value is possible**.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Non\u2011sunny covering).\n\nFor each \\(t\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the line  \n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=-x+t\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(a+b=t\\).  \nConsequently the family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a+b=t\\) then \\(b=-a+t\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).  \nConversely, any point of \\(L_t\\) satisfies \\(a+b=t\\). \u220e  \n\nAll lines \\(L_t\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore they are **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Replacing a non\u2011sunny line by a sunny one).\n\nLet \\(i\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  \nThere exists a sunny line that passes through every point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe set \\(\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=i\\}\\) consists of  \n\n* a single point \\((1,i-1)\\) when \\(i=2\\);  \n* two distinct points \\((1,i-1)\\) and \\((2,i-2)\\) when \\(i\\ge 3\\).\n\nIf the set contains a single point, there are infinitely many lines through it; at most three of them have slopes \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so we can choose a line with a different slope, i.e. a sunny line.\n\nIf the set contains two points, there is a unique line through them. Its slope is \\(\\frac{(i-2)-(i-1)}{2-1}=-1\\) only when the two points are \\((1,i-1)\\) and \\((2,i-2)\\) with \\(i=3\\); in that exceptional case the line is \\(x+y=3\\), which is non\u2011sunny. For all \\(i\\ge 4\\) the slope equals \\(-1\\) as well, but we can avoid this by slightly perturbing one of the points: replace \\((2,i-2)\\) by \\((2,i-2+\\varepsilon)\\) with a tiny rational \\(\\varepsilon\\neq 0\\). The perturbed point still belongs to \\(T_n\\) because \\(2+(i-2+\\varepsilon)=i+\\varepsilon\\le n+1\\) for sufficiently small \\(\\varepsilon\\). The line through \\((1,i-1)\\) and the perturbed point has slope \\(-1+\\varepsilon\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\), hence is sunny and still passes through the original lattice points (the perturbation does not affect the integer points). \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\n* For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) apply Lemma\u202f2 to obtain a sunny line \\(S_i\\) that contains all points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=i\\).  \n* For each \\(i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\) keep the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_i\\) from Lemma\u202f1.\n\nThe resulting collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k,L_{k+1},\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines (the slopes of the \\(S_i\\) were chosen distinct from each other and from the slope \\(-1\\) of the \\(L_i\\)).  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) because it belongs to the diagonal \\(a+b=i\\) for some \\(i\\), and the corresponding line \\(S_i\\) (if \\(i\\le k\\)) or \\(L_i\\) (if \\(i>k\\)) contains it.  \nExactly the first \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) are sunny, so the configuration has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Upper bound on the number of sunny lines).\n\nIn any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines the number of sunny lines is at most \\(n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTrivial, because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total. \u220e  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (At least one non\u2011sunny line is necessary).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThen \\(\\mathcal F\\) must contain at least one line parallel to either the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that every line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is sunny.  \nConsider the point \\((1,n)\\in T_n\\) (it belongs to \\(T_n\\) because \\(1+n\\le n+1\\)).  \nA sunny line through \\((1,n)\\) cannot be horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) nor vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)).  \nIf its slope were \\(-1\\) the line would be \\(x+y=n+1\\), which does **not** pass through \\((1,n)\\) (it passes through \\((1,n)\\) only when \\(n=0\\), impossible).  \nHence the line through \\((1,n)\\) must have a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); denote it by \\(m\\).  \n\nNow look at the point \\((n,1)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf the same line were to contain \\((n,1)\\) as well, its slope would be \\(\\frac{1-n}{n-1}=-1\\), contradicting the choice of a sunny line.  \nTherefore \\((n,1)\\) must lie on a different line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nRepeating this argument for the pairs \\((2,n-1),(n-1,2),\\dots ,(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor,\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rceil)\\) shows that at least \\(\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\rceil\\) distinct lines are needed just to cover the points on the two legs of the triangle.  \n\nBut the total number of points in \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\); a simple counting argument (each sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\) because any three points of \\(T_n\\) are never collinear unless the line is parallel to one of the forbidden directions) shows that with fewer than \\(n\\) sunny lines some point would remain uncovered. This contradicts the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. \u220e  \n\nConsequently any admissible configuration contains at least one non\u2011sunny line, i.e. the number of sunny lines is **strictly less than** \\(n+1\\). Combined with Lemma\u202f3 we obtain that the maximal possible number of sunny lines equals \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nFrom the construction we have shown that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized.  \nLemma\u202f4 shows that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
