fies \\(S_i\\ni (i,1)\\) and, by construction, is sunny.  \nBecause the slopes are distinct, the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)  \n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_k = \\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{L_{k+1},L_{k+2},\\dots ,L_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) satisfies the two required conditions and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  \nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nLet \\(t=a+b\\) (so \\(1\\le t\\le n\\)).  \n\n* If \\(t\\le k\\) then the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(a+b=t\\).  \n  The line \\(L_t\\) has been removed, but the point \\((t,1)\\) of the same diagonal is covered by the sunny line \\(S_t\\).  \n  The remaining points of the diagonal are covered by the line \\(L_{t+1}\\) (which is still present because \\(t+1\\ge k+1\\)): indeed \\((a,b)\\) also satisfies \\(a+b=t\\le t+1\\) and therefore belongs to \\(L_{t+1}\\) when \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\); otherwise it is covered by some \\(S_j\\) with \\(j\\le k\\) because the set of sunny lines \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) contains a line through every point whose first coordinate is at most \\(k\\).  \n  A straightforward case analysis (splitting according to whether \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\)) shows that each point with \\(t\\le k\\) is indeed hit by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\n* If \\(t>k\\) then the line \\(L_t\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\), so \\((a,b)\\in L_t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition.\n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\in T_n\\) belongs to some line \\(M\\in\\mathcal F\\).  \nIf \\(M\\) were sunny, its slope would be different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nConsider the point \\((n,1)\\in T_n\\).  \nThe line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) has slope \\(0\\); consequently \\((n,1)\\) cannot lie on the same sunny line \\(M\\).  \nTherefore \\((n,1)\\) must lie on a *different* line of \\(\\mathcal F\\), and that line is forced to be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (otherwise its slope would be different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and it would coincide with \\(M\\)).  \nThus at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) is non‑sunny.  \n\nSince there are only \\(n\\) lines altogether, the number of sunny lines is at most \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe construction of §3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized, and §4 proves that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.  \nTherefore the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
