=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) is on \\(D\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).  \n\n   If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then \\(b\\le k\\) implies \\(a\\ge 2\\). Set  \n\n   \\[\n   b' = n+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have \\(b'\\le n-1\\); because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b'\\ge b\\ge 1\\).  \n   Moreover \\(b'\\ge k+1\\): indeed, if \\(b'\\le k\\) then \\(a = n+1-b' \\ge n+1-k > n\\), contradicting \\(a\\le n\\).  \n   Hence \\(b'\\) belongs to the index set \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), and the horizontal line \\(H_{b'}\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).  \n   Finally, \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=b = n+1-a - (b'-b)\\); but by definition of \\(b'\\) we have \\(a+b' = n+1\\), i.e. the point \\((a,b')\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).  \n   Since the horizontal line \\(H_{b'}\\) consists of all points with ordinate \\(b'\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) is **not** on \\(H_{b'}\\).  \n   However, because \\(b\\le b'\\) and the set of points with a given abscissa \\(a\\) in \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\{(a,1),\\dots ,(a,b')\\}\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered either by the diagonal \\(D\\) (when \\(a+b=n+1\\)) or by the horizontal line \\(H_{b'}\\) after we observe that the diagonal together with the horizontals \\(H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) already cover every point whose sum is strictly less than \\(n+1\\).  \n   Indeed, for any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b<n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n-1\\). If \\(b\\ge k+1\\) we are in the first case; if \\(b\\le k\\) then \\(a\\ge n+1-b\\ge k+1\\), so \\(a\\ge k+1\\). Consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{a}\\) (which is present because \\(a\\ge k+1\\)).  \n\n   Summarising, every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\n6. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Any admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \n   The construction above shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Hence the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n\\), and all intermediate values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A basic non‑sunny covering  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n-1),\\qquad \nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1},D\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n\\(H_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, \\(D\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore every line of \\(\\mathcal H\\) is **non‑sunny**.\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then either \\(b\\le n-1\\) and \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\), or \\(b=n\\) and necessarily \\(a=1\\), whence \\((a,b)\\in D\\).  \nThus \\(\\mathcal H\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains **no** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Sunny lines that will replace horizontals  \n\nChoose distinct rational numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_{n-1}\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nc_j=j-m_j j,\\qquad \nS_j:\\; y=m_j x + c_j .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nS_j\\ni (j,1)\\quad\\text{since}\\quad m_j j + c_j = j .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m_j\\neq 0,-1\\), each \\(S_j\\) is **sunny**, and the distinct slopes guarantee that the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\) are pairwise different.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\)  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n\\).*  \nChoose an additional rational slope \\(m_n\\neq 0,-1\\) distinct from the previous ones and define \\(S_n\\) analogously.  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and clearly covers \\(T_n\\) because each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(S_a\\) (the line \\(S_a\\) passes through \\((a,1)\\) and, having a slope different from \\(-1\\), meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) only at that point; however the set \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\}\\) together with the diagonal \\(D\\) is not needed – we simply note that the case \\(k=n\\) is obtained by taking the construction of the next paragraph with \\(k=n-1\\) and then replacing the remaining horizontal line \\(H_{n-1}\\) by a sunny line \\(S_n\\).)\n\n*If \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal C_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{D\\}\\cup\\{H_{k+1},H_{k+2},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_j\\)’s) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the diagonal and the remaining horizontals).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\ge k+1\\).*  \nThen the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b\\le k\\).*  \nIf \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\nIf \\(a+b<n+1\\) then \\(a\\ge 2\\). Set  \n\n\\[\nb' = n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have \\(b'\\le n-1\\); because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b'\\ge b\\).  \nMoreover \\(b'\\ge k+1\\): indeed, if \\(b'\\le k\\) then \\(a = n+1-b' \\ge n+1-k > n\\), contradicting \\(a\\le n\\).  \nHence \\(b'\\) is one of the indices of the retained horizontals, i.e. \\(H_{b'}\\in\\mathcal C_k\\).  \n\nNow \\(a+b' = n+1\\), so the point \\((a,b')\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).  \nSince the horizontal line \\(H_{b'}\\) consists of all points with ordinate \\(b'\\), the segment of the diagonal between \\((a,b')\\) and \\((a,b)\\) is vertical; consequently the point \\((a,b)\\) is also on the same vertical line \\(x=a\\) and, because \\(b\\le b'\\), it is reached by moving **downwards** from the diagonal along that vertical line.  \nThe diagonal together with the horizontal \\(H_{b'}\\) therefore cover the whole column \\(\\{(a,1),\\dots ,(a,b')\\}\\); in particular \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the union of \\(D\\) and \\(H_{b'}\\), both of which are members of \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Distinctness and non‑sunny nature of the non‑sunny lines  \n\nThe horizontals \\(H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are pairwise distinct and none of them is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\); the diagonal \\(D\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny, and it is not equal to any horizontal line because its slope is \\(-1\\) whereas each horizontal has slope \\(0\\).  \nHence all \\(n\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nA configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThe construction above shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Consequently the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]"
