en \\(\\mathcal C_n=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and clearly covers \\(T_n\\) because each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on the line \\(S_a\\).”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nThe claim that “\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_a\\)” depends on the false statement that \\(S_a\\) contains \\((a,1)\\). Since \\(S_a\\) actually contains \\((a,a)\\), there is no guarantee that an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq a\\) lies on \\(S_a\\). Consequently the argument that \\(\\mathcal C_n\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\) is unfounded.\n\n---\n\n#### 5. Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\n> “Define \\(\\mathcal C_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{D\\}\\cup\\{H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\}\\).”\n\nThe definition itself is syntactically fine, but its intended covering property depends on the faulty properties of the \\(S_j\\) established above. Therefore the claimed covering for \\(k\\ge 1\\) is not justified.\n\n---\n\n#### 6. Verification that \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\n> **Case 1:** “If \\(b\\ge k+1\\) then the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).”\n\n*Correct.* This part is valid because the horizontals are indeed in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) for those indices.\n\n> **Case 2:** “If \\(b\\le k\\). If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\)… If \\(a+b<n+1\\) then \\(a\\ge 2\\). Set \\(b' = n+1-a\\). … Hence \\(b'\\) is one of the indices of the retained horizontals, i.e. \\(H_{b'}\\in\\mathcal C_k\\).”\n\n*Justification Gap.*  \nThe argument that \\(b'\\ge k+1\\) is presented as:\n\n> “Indeed, if \\(b'\\le k\\) then \\(a = n+1-b' \\ge n+1-k > n\\), contradicting \\(a\\le n\\).”\n\nThis reasoning is flawed. From \\(b'\\le k\\) we obtain \\(a = n+1-b' \\ge n+1-k\\). The inequality \\(n+1-k > n\\) holds **only when \\(k<1\\)**, which is not generally true for arbitrary \\(k\\). For example, with \\(n=6\\) and \\(k=3\\), we have \\(n+1-k = 4\\), which is not greater than 