            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0\\quad(\\text{the }x\\text{-axis}),\\qquad \n\\ell_y:\\;x=0\\quad(\\text{the }y\\text{-axis}),\\qquad \n\\ell_{-1}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line must have a slope different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) and must not be vertical.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A covering with no sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n-1),\\qquad \nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1},D\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nEach \\(H_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, and \\(D\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_{-1}\\); consequently every line of \\(\\mathcal H\\) is non‑sunny.\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then either \\(b\\le n-1\\) and \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\), or \\(b=n\\) (which forces \\(a=1\\)) and \\((a,b)\\in D\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal H\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains **zero** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Sunny lines that will replace some horizontals  \n\nChoose distinct rational numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_1,m_2,\\dots ,m_{n-1}\\in\\mathbb Q\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) set  \n\n\\[\nc_j = j - m_j j ,\\qquad \nS_j:\\; y = m_j x + c_j .\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nm_j\\cdot j + c_j = m_j j + (j-m_j j)=j ,\n\\]\n\nso the point \\((j,1)\\) lies on \\(S_j\\).  \nBecause \\(m_j\\neq 0,-1\\), each \\(S_j\\) is sunny, and the distinct slopes guarantee that the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-1}\\) are pairwise different.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Configurations with a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(k=n\\).*  \nChoose an additional rational slope \\(m_n\\neq 0,-1\\) distinct from the previous ones and define \\(S_n\\) analogously.  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n=\\{S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nTo cover the points of \\(T_n\\) we keep the diagonal \\(D\\) (which is non‑sunny) and replace one of the horizontals \\(H_j\\) (say \\(H_{n-1}\\)) by the extra sunny line \\(S_n\\).  Thus the final family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n'=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-1},D,S_n\\}\n\\]\n\nhas exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines and, as shown in the next paragraph, covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal C_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{D\\}\\cup\\{H_{k+1},H_{k+2},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\)) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the diagonal and the remaining horizontals).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Verification that \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(b\\ge k+1\\).*  \nThe horizontal line \\(H_b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(b\\le k\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\in\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\n*Case 3: \\(b\\le k\\) and \\(a+b<n+1\\).*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nb' = n+1-a .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) we have \\(b'\\le n\\); the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields \\(b'\\ge b\\).  \nMoreover \\(a\\ge 2\\) (otherwise \\(a=1\\) would force \\(b=n\\), contradicting \\(b\\le k\\le n-1\\)), hence \\(b'\\le n-1\\).\n\nWe claim that \\(b'\\ge k+1\\).  \nIf \\(b'\\le k\\) then  \n\n\\[\na = n+1-b' \\ge n+1-k > n ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible because \\(a\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  Therefore \\(b'\\ge k+1\\).\n\nConsequently the horizontal line \\(H_{b'}\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).  \nBy construction \\(a+b' = n+1\\), so the point \\((a,b')\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).  \nThe vertical segment joining \\((a,b)\\) to \\((a,b')\\) is contained in \\(T_n\\); its upper endpoint belongs to \\(D\\) and its lower endpoint belongs to the same vertical line \\(x=a\\).  Since the diagonal \\(D\\) and the horizontal line \\(H_{b'}\\) together contain the whole vertical segment (the diagonal supplies the upper endpoint and the horizontal supplies the lower endpoint), the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\nAll possibilities for \\((a,b)\\) fall into one of the three cases, so every element of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Distinctness of the lines  \n\nThe horizontals \\(H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\) are pairwise distinct and have slope \\(0\\); the diagonal \\(D\\) has slope \\(-1\\); each sunny line \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(m_j\\neq 0,-1\\).  Hence no line of \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) coincides with another, and \\(|\\mathcal C_k|=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Upper bound and conclusion  \n\nAny admissible configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThe constructions above show that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nTherefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
