dden direction, which is allowed \u2013 actually we may have many of\n   each direction; the crucial point is the following counting argument).\n\n   Consider a fixed row \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-1\\).  \n   The points of \\(T_n\\) on this row are  \n\n   \\[\n   R_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\},\\qquad |R_b|=n+1-b .\n   \\]\n\n   * A horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (if present) covers the whole set \\(R_b\\).  \n   * Any other non\u2011sunny line (vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) can contain **at most one**\n     point of \\(R_b\\) (by Lemma\u202f1).  \n   * Each sunny line can also contain at most one point of \\(R_b\\) (Lemma\u202f1).\n\n   Hence, if the row \\(y=b\\) is **not** covered by a horizontal line, the total\n   number of points of \\(R_b\\) that can be covered by the remaining\n   \\(n-1\\) lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k-1)\\;+\\;k \\;=\\; n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   (the term \\(n-k-1\\) counts the non\u2011sunny lines other than a possible\n   horizontal line, each contributing at most one point; the term \\(k\\) counts\n   the sunny lines).  \n   For the row \\(b=1\\) we have \\(|R_1|=n\\); for \\(b=2\\) we have \\(|R_2|=n-1\\), etc.\n   In order to cover the longest row \\(R_1\\) we must therefore have a horizontal\n   line \\(y=1\\) **or** we need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines, which is impossible\n   because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total.  Consequently a horizontal line\n   must be present for the first row.\n\n   Repeating the same argument for the second row \\(y=2\\) shows that we also\n   need a horizontal line \\(y=2\\) unless we already have at least \\(n-1\\) lines\n   other than sunny lines, which again cannot happen when \\(k\\ge n-1\\).\n   Continuing inductively we obtain:\n\n   > **Claim.** At least \\(n-2\\) rows must be covered by horizontal lines.\n\n   Since there are only \\(n\\) lines altogether, at most two of them can be\n   non\u2011horizontal.  Those two may be taken, for instance, as a vertical line\n   and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Therefore the number of sunny lines\n   satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)**  \n\n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) in the stated range.  \n   We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that\n   cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Horizontal lines.*  \n   Take the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2-k).\n   \\]\n\n   These are \\(n-2-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines and they cover completely the rows\n   \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2-k\\).\n\n   *Diagonal line.*  \n   Add the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  It covers, for each\n   row \\(y=b\\) with \\(b\\ge n-1-k\\), the unique point \\((a,b)\\) satisfying\n   \\(a+b=n+1\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For the remaining rows \\(y=b\\) with \\(b=n-1-k,\\dots ,n-1\\) we still have to\n   cover the points that are not on \\(D\\).  In row \\(y=b\\) the set of uncovered\n   points is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b,\\;a\\neq n+1-b\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Its cardinality is \\(n-b\\).  Choose \\(k\\) distinct points, one from each of\n   the first \\(k\\) of these rows (for instance \\((1,n-1), (2,n-2),\\dots ,\n   (k,n-k)\\)).  Through each chosen point draw a line with slope different from\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for concreteness we may take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{2}x + \\left(b_i+\\frac{1}{2}a_i\\right)\n   \\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\n   \\]\n\n   where \\((a_i,b_i)\\) is the chosen point.  All these lines are sunny,\n   distinct (different slopes or different intercepts), and each of them\n   covers exactly the point \\((a_i,b_i)\\) of its row.\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2-k) \\;+\\; 1 \\;+\\; k = n .\n   \\]\n\n   The number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).  By construction every point of\n   \\(T_n\\) lies either on a horizontal line, on the diagonal \\(D\\), or on one of\n   the sunny lines, so the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n   Hence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n\n   The upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) together with the explicit construction for each\n   admissible \\(k\\) shows that the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is\n   precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1  \nIf a line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, then it meets each horizontal line\n\\(y=b\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  Write the line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq 0\\).  Solving together with\n\\(y=b\\) gives the unique point \\((\\frac{b-c}{m},b)\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2  \nIf a line is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (resp. to \\(x+y=0\\)), then it meets\neach vertical line \\(x=a\\) (resp. each anti\u2011diagonal \\(x+y=c\\)) in at most one\npoint.\n\n*Proof.*  Analogous to Lemma\u202f1. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(T_n\\) and contains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are\nnon\u2011sunny, i.e. each is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nConsider a row  \n\n\\[\nR_b=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\},\\qquad 1\\le b\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nIf the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\) then the whole row is\ncovered.  Otherwise, by Lemma\u202f1 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most one\npoint of \\(R_b\\), and each sunny line also contributes at most one point of\n\\(R_b\\).  Hence, without a horizontal line, at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-k-1)+k = n-1\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(R_b\\) can be covered (the term \\(n-k-1\\) counts the non\u2011sunny lines\ndifferent from a possible horizontal line).\n\nFor the first row \\(R_1\\) we have \\(|R_1|=n\\); therefore a horizontal line\n\\(y=1\\) must be present in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Removing that line, the same argument\napplied to the second row \\(R_2\\) (which has \\(|R_2|=n-1\\)) forces the presence\nof the horizontal line \\(y=2\\).  Continuing inductively we obtain that the\nhorizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) must all belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus at least \\(n-2\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are horizontal.  Consequently at most\n\\(2\\) lines can be non\u2011horizontal, and those two may be sunny or not.  Hence\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  We build \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nones.\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nTake the \\(n-2-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\;y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2-k).\n\\]\n\nThese cover completely the rows \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-2-k\\).\n\n*Diagonal line.*  \nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIt is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  For each row\n\\(y=b\\) with \\(b\\ge n-1-k\\) the point \\((a,b)=(n+1-b,b)\\) lies on \\(D\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor the rows \\(y=b\\) with \\(b=n-1-k,\\dots ,n-1\\) the points not on \\(D\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-b\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose one such point from each of the first \\(k\\) of these rows; for definiteness\nlet  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\,n-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nThrough \\(P_i\\) draw a line with slope \\(-\\frac12\\) (any slope different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) would do):\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = -\\frac12 x + \\Bigl(n-i+\\frac12 i\\Bigr)\n      = -\\frac12 x + \\Bigl(n-\\frac i2\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(S_i\\) are sunny, distinct (they have the same slope but different\nintercepts), and each contains exactly the chosen point \\(P_i\\) of its row.\n\n*Counting.*  \nNumber of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-2-k)}_{\\text{horizontal}}+\n\\underbrace{1}_{D}+\n\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}} = n .\n\\]\n\nNumber of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).  Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either\nto a horizontal line, to \\(D\\), or to one of the sunny lines, so the covering\ncondition holds.\n\nThus a configuration with any prescribed \\(k\\) in the interval\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final answer  \n\nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,}.\n\\]\n\nNo configuration can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines, while for each\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration exists, as shown above."
