lope belongs to the set \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  All integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall construct \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\) such that  \n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with positive integers \\(a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n##### 1.1  The non\u2011sunny part  \n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;v_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k)\\;}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(v_j\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore it is **not** sunny.  \nThe lines are distinct because they have different \\(x\\)-intercepts.\n\n---\n\n##### 1.2  The sunny part  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define a line \\(\\sigma_i\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\sigma_i:\\; y-x = 1-(n-k+i)\\;}\\qquad\\text{(equivalently }y = x + 1-(n-k+i)\\text{).}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\sigma_i\\) is \\(1\\); since \\(1\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), each \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny.  \nAll \\(\\sigma_i\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nObserve that \\(\\sigma_i\\) passes through the lattice point  \n\n\\[\nP_i = (n-k+i,\\;1).\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n1 = (n-k+i) + 1-(n-k+i).\n\\]\n\nMoreover, because the slope is \\(1\\), the integer points on \\(\\sigma_i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{\\, (n-k+i+t,\\;1+t)\\mid t\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor such a point we have  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(n-k+i+t\\bigr)+\\bigl(1+t\\bigr)=n+1+i\\le n+1+k\\le n+1+n=n+1,\n\\]\n\nso the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is satisfied precisely for those \\(t\\) with  \n\n\\[\nt\\le k-i .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the set of lattice points of \\(\\sigma_i\\) that lie inside the triangle\n\\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\, (n-k+i,\\;1),\\;(n-k+i+1,\\;2),\\dots ,(n-i+1,\\;k-i+1)\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n##### 1.3  Verification of the covering condition  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\) be any lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\):* then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(v_a\\) because \\(v_a\\) is defined by \\(x=a\\).\n\n*If \\(a> n-k\\):* write \\(a=n-k+i+t\\) with \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and \\(t\\ge 0\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-k+i+t)=k-i+1-t .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b\\ge 1\\), the only integer \\(t\\) that can satisfy both inequalities is \\(t=b-1\\).  \nThus \\(a=n-k+i+(b-1)\\) and consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(\\sigma_i\\).\n\nHence every admissible lattice point belongs to at least one line of the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_k=\\{v_1,\\dots ,v_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{\\sigma_1,\\dots ,\\sigma_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\sigma_i\\) are sunny.  \nTherefore for the chosen \\(k\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  No configuration can have \\(k>n\\)\n\nA configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nSince a line is either sunny or not sunny, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines.  \nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the existence result of Part\u202f1 (which shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised) with the obvious necessary condition \\(k\\le n\\) from Part\u202f2, we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For a given }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
