gr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (sunny lines)\n\nA line whose slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(y = \\frac{p}{q}x + c\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q\\neq0\\); the conditions “not horizontal”, “not vertical” and “not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)” are equivalent to \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).\n\nAssume two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) lie on the line.  \nSubtracting the equations gives  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1 = \\frac{p}{q}(a_2-a_1).\n\\]\n\nHence \\(q\\mid (a_2-a_1)\\) and \\(p\\mid (b_2-b_1)\\).  \nThere exists a non‑zero integer \\(t\\) such that  \n\n\\[\na_2-a_1 = q t,\\qquad b_2-b_1 = p t .\n\\]\n\nNow consider the sums of the coordinates:\n\n\\[\n(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)= (q t)+(p t)= t(p+q). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth sums lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|t(p+q)|\\le n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\neq -q\\), we have \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\).  \nIf \\(|t|\\ge1\\) then (3) forces \\(|t(p+q)|\\ge1\\).  \nBut (2) shows that the two sums differ by a non‑zero multiple of \\(|p+q|\\), which is at least \\(1\\).  \nSince the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) contains at most \\(n\\) distinct integers, the only way (3) can hold with \\(|t|\\ge1\\) is that the two sums are equal, i.e. \\(t(p+q)=0\\).  \nBecause \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\), this forces \\(t=0\\), contradicting the assumption that the points are distinct.  \n\nThus no two distinct points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (non‑sunny lines)\n\nIf a line is vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\) then it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n*Vertical* \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\): admissible \\(b\\) satisfy \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) such points.  \n\n*Horizontal* \\(y=c\\) is symmetric.  \n\n*Slope \\(-1\\)* lines have equation \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(d\\) the admissible points are \\((a,d-a)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\); their number is \\(d-1\\le n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 3 (necessary bound on \\(k\\))\n\nIf \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nBy Lemma 2 the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n(n-1)k\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral, the claimed bound follows. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 4 (construction for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nm=n-2k\\qquad(\\text{note that }m\\ge1\\text{ because }k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}).\n\\]\n\n**(i) Non‑sunny lines.**  \nTake the following \\(n-2k=m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m,\n\\]\n\nand the following \\(k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=k .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are distinct and none is sunny.\n\n**(ii) Points still uncovered after (i).**  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is not covered by the vertical lines iff \\(a\\ge m+1\\); it is not covered by the horizontal lines iff \\(b\\ge k+1\\).  \nThus the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge m+1,\\;b\\ge k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\((a,b)\\in U\\) define  \n\n\\[\na' = a-m,\\qquad b' = b-k .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(a',b'\\ge1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na'+b' = a+b-(m+k)\\le (n+1)-(n-2k+k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(2\\le a'+b'\\le k+1\\).  \nHence each \\((a,b)\\in U\\) lies on a line of slope \\(-1\\) of the form  \n\n\\[\nx+y = m+k+d = n-k+d,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(d=a'+b'\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).\n\n**(iii) Sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le k+1\\) choose a sunny line \\(\\ell_d\\) that passes through **exactly one** point of \\(U\\) whose shifted sum equals \\(d\\).  \nBecause a sunny line can meet \\(U\\subseteq T_n\\) in at most one point (Lemma 1), such a choice is possible.  \nAn explicit convenient choice is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\; y = \\frac{1}{d}\\,x + \\bigl(d-(m)\\bigr) .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{1}{d}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_d\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_d\\mid d=2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n**(iv) Coverage.**  \n- The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le m\\).  \n- The horizontal lines cover all points with second coordinate \\(\\le k\\).  \n- Any remaining point belongs to \\(U\\); by construction it lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_d\\) corresponding to its shifted sum \\(d\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.  \nExactly \\(k\\) of these lines (\\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{k+1}\\)) are sunny, the others are not. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (final answer)\n\nFor an integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nProposition 3 shows that any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  \nProposition 4 exhibits, for every integer \\(k\\) in this range, a concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence the listed values are precisely the admissible ones. ∎"
