.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (sunny lines)\n\nA line whose slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in the form \\(y = \\frac{p}{q}x + c\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q\\neq0\\); the conditions \u201cnot horizontal\u201d, \u201cnot vertical\u201d and \u201cnot parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)\u201d are equivalent to \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(p\\neq -q\\).\n\nAssume two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) lie on the line.  \nSubtracting the equations gives  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1 = \\frac{p}{q}(a_2-a_1).\n\\]\n\nHence \\(q\\mid (a_2-a_1)\\) and \\(p\\mid (b_2-b_1)\\).  \nThere exists a non\u2011zero integer \\(t\\) such that  \n\n\\[\na_2-a_1 = q t,\\qquad b_2-b_1 = p t .\n\\]\n\nNow consider the sums of the coordinates:\n\n\\[\n(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)= (q t)+(p t)= t(p+q). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth sums lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|t(p+q)|\\le n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\neq -q\\), we have \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\).  \nIf \\(|t|\\ge1\\) then (3) forces \\(|t(p+q)|\\ge1\\).  \nBut (2) shows that the two sums differ by a non\u2011zero multiple of \\(|p+q|\\), which is at least \\(1\\).  \nSince the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) contains at most \\(n\\) distinct integers, the only way (3) can hold with \\(|t|\\ge1\\) is that the two sums are equal, i.e. \\(t(p+q)=0\\).  \nBecause \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\), this forces \\(t=0\\), contradicting the assumption that the points are distinct.  