rtical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  (Proof as in the original solution.)\n\n4. **Necessary condition on \\(k\\).**  \n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   points, and by Lemma 2 the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary bound  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sufficiency (construction).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4).  Write \\(m=n-2k\\;( \\ge 1)\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m,\n   \\]\n\n   and the \\(k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=k .\n   \\]\n\n   *Uncovered points.*  \n   After these \\(n-2k\\) lines the points still uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge m+1,\\;b\\ge k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\((a,b)\\in U\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   a'=a-m,\\qquad b'=b-k .\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(a',b'\\ge1\\) and  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le a'+b'\\le k+1 . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le k+1\\) choose a sunny line \\(\\ell_d\\) that passes through **exactly one** point of \\(U\\) whose shifted sum \\(a'+b'\\) equals \\(d\\).  \n   Because of Lemma 1 a sunny line can meet \\(U\\subseteq T_n\\) in at most one point, so such a choice is possible.  \n   An explicit convenient choice is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_d:\\; y=\\frac{1}{d}\\,x+\\bigl(d-m\\bigr) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{1}{d}\\) is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_d\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_d\\mid d=2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   – The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le m\\).  \n   – The horizontal lines cover all points with second coordinate \\(\\le k\\).  \n   – If \\((a,b)\\in U\\) then, by (5), \\(a'+b'=d\\) for a unique \\(d\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\); by construction the sunny line \\(\\ell_d\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  The lines are distinct, so the construction works for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (4) and the explicit construction for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying it together give the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nA line whose slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (i.e. a *sunny* line) contains at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in reduced form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+c ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q\\neq0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\]\n\nwith the sunny conditions \\(p\\neq0\\) (not horizontal), \\(q\\neq0\\) (not vertical) and \\(p\\neq -q\\) (not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nIf \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) are two distinct points on the line, then for some non‑zero integer \\(t\\)\n\n\\[\na_2-a_1=q\\,t,\\qquad b_2-b_1=p\\,t .\n\\]\n\nHence the sums satisfy  \n\n\\[\n(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)=t(p+q). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is sunny, \\(p+q\\neq0\\). Moreover \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) imply \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) (the only way to have \\(|p+q|=1\\) would be \\((p,q)=(1,0)\\) or \\((0,1)\\), which correspond to the forbidden horizontal or vertical directions).  \n\nAll sums \\(a+b\\) of points of \\(T_n\\) lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\); therefore the absolute difference of two distinct such sums is at most \\(n-1\\). From (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|t|\\le\\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n\\[\n|t|_{\\max}+1\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (non‑sunny lines)\n\nIf a line is vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\) then it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Vertical* \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\): admissible \\(b\\) satisfy \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\), giving at most \\(n\\) points.  \n*Horizontal* is analogous.  \n*Slope \\(-1\\)* lines have equation \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\); for a fixed \\(d\\) the admissible points are \\((a,d-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\), i.e. \\(d-1\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 3 (necessary bound on the number of sunny lines)\n\nIf \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  \nBy Lemma 2 the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.  \nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA short algebraic manipulation yields \\(k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\), and since \\(k\\) is integral we obtain the claimed bound. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proposition 4 (construction for every admissible \\(k\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nThen there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSet \\(m=n-2k\\) (note that \\(m\\ge1\\) because \\(k\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\)).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Take the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m,\n   \\]\n\n   and the \\(k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=k .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-2k\\) lines are distinct and none of them is sunny.\n\n2. **Points still uncovered.**  \n   After step 1 the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge m+1,\\;b\\ge k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\((a,b)\\in U\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   a'=a-m,\\qquad b'=b-k .\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(a',b'\\ge1\\) and, using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   a'+b' = a+b-(m+k)\\le (n+1)-(n-2k+k)=k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le a'+b'\\le k+1 . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le k+1\\) choose a sunny line \\(\\ell_d\\) that passes through **exactly one** point of \\(U\\) whose shifted sum \\(a'+b'\\) equals \\(d\\).  \n   Lemma 1 guarantees that a sunny line can meet \\(U\\subseteq T_n\\) in at most one point, so such a choice is possible.  \n   An explicit convenient choice is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_d:\\; y=\\frac{1}{d}\\,x+\\bigl(d-m\\bigr) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{1}{d}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_d\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_d\\mid d=2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\n4. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   - The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le m\\).  \n   - The horizontal lines cover all points with second coordinate \\(\\le k\\).  \n   - Let \\((a,b)\\in U\\). Then \\(a'+b'=d\\) for a unique \\(d\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) by (6); by construction the sunny line \\(\\ell_d\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.  \n   Exactly \\(k\\) of these lines (\\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{k+1}\\)) are sunny, the others are not. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (final answer)\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nProposition 3 shows that any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy the bound (4).  \nProposition 4 exhibits, for every integer \\(k\\) obeying that bound, a concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence the listed integers are precisely the admissible values of \\(k\\). ∎"
