lusions  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) lie on the same line (2) then, for some\nnon\u2011zero integer \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\na_2-a_1=q\\,t,\\qquad b_2-b_1=p\\,t . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities gives the relation for the sums of the\ncoordinates:\n\n\\[\n(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)=t(p+q). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (3) we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\); moreover \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\) and\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) imply  \n\n\\[\n|p+q|\\ge 1 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 a sunny line meets at most one point of each diagonal  \n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most one** point of each diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_s\\) for some fixed sum \\(s\\).  \nThen \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2=s\\), so the left\u2011hand side of (5) is zero.  \nConsequently \\(t(p+q)=0\\). Since \\(t\\neq0\\) we would have \\(p+q=0\\), contradicting (3). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 a sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of the set \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 a sunny line can meet each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point,\nhence the number of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the line equals the number of\ndifferent diagonals it meets.  \nAll possible sums \\(a+b\\) lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\), whose length is\n\\(n-1\\).  \nFrom (5) the differences between the sums of any two points on the line are\nmultiples of \\(|p+q|\\ge 1\\); therefore the set of attainable sums on the line\nforms an arithmetic progression with step at least\u202f1 inside an interval of\nlength \\(n-1\\).  Such a progression can contain at most \\(n\\) terms. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 a non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) meets the set in the points\n\\((c,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.\nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line.\nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\); it\ncontains exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Proposition\u202f4 \u2013 necessary bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\)\nand contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 the sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot n\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f3 the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover at most \\((n-k)n\\)\npoints.  Hence\n\n\\[\nk n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side equals \\(n^{2}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nn^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Proposition\u202f5 \u2013 construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nAssume that \\(k\\) satisfies (7).  Write  \n\n\\[\nm=n-2k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge 1).\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nthat together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 7.1  The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake the following \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m,\n\\]\n\nand the following \\(k\\) horizontal li