            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) are exactly the integer points lying on or below the line\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) in the first quadrant.  \nIts cardinality is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\]\n\nthe “diagonal’’ of points whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\).  \nEach \\(D_s\\) is non‑empty, \\(|D_s|=s-1\\), and the family \\(\\{D_s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\}\\) partitions \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Geometry of a sunny line  \n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nWrite a sunny line in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+c ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q\\neq0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwith the three exclusions  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) lie on the same line (2) then, for some\nnon‑zero integer \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\na_2-a_1=q\\,t,\\qquad b_2-b_1=p\\,t . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two equalities gives the relation for the sums of the\ncoordinates:\n\n\\[\n(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)=t(p+q). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (3) we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\); moreover \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\) and\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) imply  \n\n\\[\n|p+q|\\ge 1 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma 1 – a sunny line meets at most one point of each diagonal  \n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. Then \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most one** point of each diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_s\\) for some fixed sum \\(s\\).  \nThen \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2=s\\), so the left‑hand side of (5) is zero.  \nConsequently \\(t(p+q)=0\\). Since \\(t\\neq0\\) we would have \\(p+q=0\\), contradicting (3). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma 2 – a sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of the set \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line can meet each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point,\nhence the number of points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the line equals the number of\ndifferent diagonals it meets.  \nAll possible sums \\(a+b\\) lie in the interval \\([2,n+1]\\), whose length is\n\\(n-1\\).  \nFrom (5) the differences between the sums of any two points on the line are\nmultiples of \\(|p+q|\\ge 1\\); therefore the set of attainable sums on the line\nforms an arithmetic progression with step at least 1 inside an interval of\nlength \\(n-1\\).  Such a progression can contain at most \\(n\\) terms. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lemma 3 – a non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) meets the set in the points\n\\((c,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) of them.\nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line.\nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\); it\ncontains exactly \\(d-1\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Proposition 4 – necessary bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\)\nand contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 2 the sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot n\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  \nBy Lemma 3 the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines cover at most \\((n-k)n\\)\npoints.  Hence\n\n\\[\nk n+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side equals \\(n^{2}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nn^{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Proposition 5 – construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nAssume that \\(k\\) satisfies (7).  Write  \n\n\\[\nm=n-2k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge 1).\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\nthat together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 7.1  The non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake the following \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m,\n\\]\n\nand the following \\(k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=k .\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny and pairwise distinct.  \nAfter adding these \\(n-2k\\) lines the points that are still uncovered are\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge m+1,\\;b\\ge k+1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 7.2  Description of the remaining points  \n\nFor \\((a,b)\\in U\\) set  \n\n\\[\na'=a-m,\\qquad b'=b-k .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and, using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b' = a+b-(m+k)\\le (n+1)-(n-2k+k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n2\\le a'+b'\\le k+1 . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus each point of \\(U\\) belongs to exactly one diagonal\n\\(D'_d=\\{(a',b')\\mid a'+b'=d\\}\\) with \\(d\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 7.3  The sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le k+1\\) we define a line\n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\; y=\\frac{1}{d}\\,x+\\bigl(d-m\\bigr). \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Why \\(\\ell_d\\) is sunny.*  \nIts slope is \\(\\frac{1}{d}\\); this is never \\(0\\), never undefined, and never\n\\(-1\\) because \\(d\\ge2\\).  Hence \\(\\ell_d\\) is sunny.\n\n*Why \\(\\ell_d\\) meets \\(U\\) in exactly one point.*  \nTake a point \\((a,b)\\in U\\) and write \\(a',b'\\) as above.  \nFrom (9) we have \\(a'+b'=d\\) for a unique \\(d\\).  Solving (10) for \\(x\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx = d\\,(y-d+m).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(y=b\\) and \\(x=a\\) and using \\(a=m+a',\\;b=k+b'\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nm+a' = d\\bigl(k+b' - d + m\\bigr)\n\\Longleftrightarrow a' = d(b'-d+1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a'+b'=d\\) we have \\(a'=d-b'\\); inserting this into the previous\nequality yields  \n\n\\[\nd-b' = d(b'-d+1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; (b'-1)(d-b')=0 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b'\\ge1\\) and \\(b'\\le d-1\\) (by (9)), the only possibility is\n\\(b'=1\\) and consequently \\(a'=d-1\\).  Hence the unique point of \\(U\\) that\nlies on \\(\\ell_d\\) is \\((a,b)=(m+d-1,\\;k+1)\\).  Different values of \\(d\\) give\ndifferent slopes, so the \\(\\ell_d\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 7.4  Verification of the covering  \n\n* The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le m\\).  \n* The horizontal lines cover all points with second coordinate \\(\\le k\\).  \n* If \\((a,b)\\in U\\) then, by (9), \\(a'+b'=d\\) for a unique\n\\(d\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\); the sunny line \\(\\ell_d\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\)\nby the construction above.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed\nlines, exactly \\(k\\) of which (\\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{k+1}\\)) are sunny.\nAll lines are distinct, completing the construction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Final theorem  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which\nthere exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane satisfying\n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one\n  of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny,\n\nis precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nProposition 4 shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy the\ninequality (7), i.e. \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).  \nProposition 5 constructs, for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence the listed integers are exactly the admissible values of \\(k\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
