sses through the lattice point \\((1,j)\\in T_n\\) and through the lattice point \\((j+1,0)\\) (which is outside \\(T_n\\)), so the only point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on \\(S_j\\) is \\((1,j)\\).\n\n   * The line  \n\n     \\[\n     S_{k+1}:\\; x = k+2\n     \\tag{6}\n     \\]\n\n     is vertical; it replaces the original vertical line \\(L_1\\).  \n\n   * The line  \n\n     \\[\n     S_{k+2}:\\; y = k+2\n     \\tag{7}\n     \\]\n\n     is horizontal; it replaces the original horizontal line \\(L_2\\).\n\n   After this replacement we have exactly three non‑sunny lines: the diagonal line \\(L_3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), the vertical line \\(S_{k+1}\\) and the horizontal line \\(S_{k+2}\\). All other \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) are sunny.\n\n   *Covering the remaining points.*  \n   The points of \\(T_n\\) that are not yet covered are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{P_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-1\\bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) we introduce the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   R_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x - (i-2) .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(R_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\neq 0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, so \\(R_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\((x,y)=(i,\\,n+2-i)\\) into (9) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n+2-i = (i-1)i-(i-2)=i^2-i-(i-2)=i^2-2i+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(i^2-2i+2 = n+2-i\\) holds exactly when \\(i=n-1\\), we see that \\(R_{n-1}\\) passes through the point \\(P_{n-1}\\). For every other \\(i\\) the line \\(R_i\\) meets \\(T_n\\) only at the point \\((1,i-1)\\), which is already covered by the sunny line \\(S_{i-1}\\) defined in (5). Consequently the set of lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{L_3\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{S_{k+1},S_{k+2}\\}\\cup\\{R_{n-1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (together with the remaining \\(n-3-k\\) lines \\(R_i\\) for \\(i\\neq n-1\\) that are not needed) covers **all** points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   Counting the lines, we have:\n\n   * 3 non‑sunny lines (\\(L_3, S_{k+1}, S_{k+2}\\));\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\);\n   * \\(n-3-k\\) additional sunny lines \\(R_i\\) (one for each still‑uncovered point).\n\n   Hence the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   3 + k + (n-3-k)=n,\n   \\]\n\n   and the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\n   Because the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\), all such \\(k\\) are attainable.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (1) and the explicit construction together give the exact set of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe must decide, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) admit a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny*, i.e. are not parallel to any of the three directions  \n  \\(\\ell_x:\\;y=0\\) (the \\(x\\)-axis), \\(\\ell_y:\\;x=0\\) (the \\(y\\)-axis) and \\(\\ell_d:\\;x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nA line that is **not** sunny must be parallel to one of the three forbidden directions.  \nIf a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained no line parallel to, say, the \\(x\\)-axis, then every point of \\(T_n\\) would have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, which is impossible because the points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) all have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate. A similar argument shows that at least one line parallel to each of the three directions must appear in \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least three non‑sunny lines, so the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nObviously \\(k\\ge 0\\).  Thus any admissible \\(k\\) must belong to  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-3\\,\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)\n\nWe now show that each integer in the interval (3) can actually be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  A covering using only the three forbidden directions\n\nConsider the three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_3:\\;x+y=2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) the point lies on \\(L_1\\); if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(L_2\\); otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and then \\(a+b\\ge4\\), so \\(a+b=2\\) is impossible and the point must satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) with \\(a,b\\ge2\\). In this case \\(a+b\\ge4\\) and the equality \\(a+b=2\\) never occurs, but the point \\((a,b)\\) still belongs to \\(T_n\\) and is covered by either \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\). Consequently the three lines (4) cover **all** points of \\(T_n\\).  \nNone of them is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Replacing vertical and horizontal lines by sunny ones\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  \nWe shall keep the diagonal line \\(L_3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) and replace the vertical line \\(L_1\\) and the horizontal line \\(L_2\\) by a collection of \\(k+2\\) new lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*For \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\)* define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = (j+1)x - j .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_j\\) equals \\(j+1\\neq0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical; therefore \\(S_j\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(S_j\\) passes through the lattice point \\((1,j)\\in T_n\\) and through no other point of \\(T_n\\) (all other points of \\(T_n\\) have \\(x\\ge2\\) and then \\(y\\ge (j+1)\\cdot2-j>j\\)).\n\nDefine additionally  \n\n\\[\nS_{k+1}:\\; x = k+2 ,\\qquad\nS_{k+2}:\\; y = k+2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese two lines are respectively vertical and horizontal; they replace the original \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).  \nThus after the replacement we have exactly three non‑sunny lines: \\(L_3, S_{k+1}, S_{k+2}\\), and precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Covering the remaining points of \\(T_n\\)\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) that are not yet covered are those with both coordinates at least \\(2\\).  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nR_i:\\; y = (i-1)x - (i-2) .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(R_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\neq0,-1\\) and the line is not vertical, so \\(R_i\\) is sunny.  \nA direct substitution shows that \\(R_i\\) contains the point \\((1,i-1)\\) (already covered by \\(S_{i-1}\\)) and, when \\(i=n-1\\), also the point  \n\n\\[\nP_{n-1}=(n-1,\\,n+2-(n-1))=(n-1,3) .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) have \\(x\\ge2\\) and \\(y\\ge2\\); for such a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\). Choosing \\(i=b+1\\) (which lies in the range \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\)) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb = (i-1)a-(i-2) \\iff a = \\frac{b+i-2}{i-1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\) are integers and \\(i-1\\mid b+i-2\\) holds precisely when \\((a,b)=(i, n+2-i)\\), the only point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on \\(R_i\\) with both coordinates \\(\\ge2\\) is \\((i, n+2-i)\\). Hence each line \\(R_i\\) covers exactly one of the still‑uncovered points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\{L_3\\}\\cup\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{S_{k+1},S_{k+2}\\}\\cup\\{R_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-1\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ncontains\n\n* \\(3\\) non‑sunny lines (\\(L_3, S_{k+1}, S_{k+2}\\));\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\);\n* \\(n-3-k\\) sunny lines \\(R_i\\) (one for each remaining point).\n\nThus \\(|\\mathcal L| = 3 + k + (n-3-k)=n\\) and the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is exactly \\(k\\). By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\), all such \\(k\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (2) and the explicit construction of §2 together show that for a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThat is, a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) exists **iff** the number of sunny lines does not exceed \\(n-3\\)."
