 non‑sunny line \\(L_t:x+y=t+1\\).  Choose any point\n\\((a,b)\\in L_t\\cap T_n\\) and any sunny line \\(S\\) through \\((a,b)\\).\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L'=(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{L_t\\})\\cup\\{S\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\mathcal L'\\) still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by \\(S\\).  The other points of the\ndiagonal \\(L_t\\cap T_n\\) are \\((a',b')\\) with \\(a'+b'=t+1\\) and\n\\((a',b')\\neq(a,b)\\).  For each such point choose the integer\n\\(j=a'-a\\;(1\\le j\\le t-1)\\).  Replace, for all these \\(j\\), the line\n\\(L_{t-j}\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nL'_{t-j}:x+y=t-j+2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a'+b'=t+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b' = (t-j+1)+(j) = t+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a',b')\\) lies on either \\(L_{t-j}\\) (if \\(j=0\\)) or on the newly\nintroduced line \\(L'_{t-j}\\) (if \\(j\\ge 1\\)).  Hence every point of\n\\(L_t\\cap T_n\\) remains covered.  All other points of \\(T_n\\) lie on\ndiagonals with index different from \\(t\\) and are untouched.  The\nresulting family still has \\(n\\) distinct lines, because the newly added\nlines have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and are not parallel to\nany line already present. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Iterated replacement).\n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}\\) of Lemma 1, for any integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nProceed by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n*Base \\(k=0\\).*  \\(\\mathcal L^{(0)}\\) satisfies the claim by Lemma 1.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\) exists with exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines.  Choose any diagonal line \\(L_t\\) among the non‑sunny lines\nin \\(\\mathcal L^{(k)}\\); such a line exists because \\(k<n\\).  Apply Lemma 2\nto replace \\(L_t\\) by a sunny line through a point of \\(L_t\\cap T_n\\).\nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L^{(k+1)}\\) has \\(k+1\\) sunny lines and\nstill covers \\(T_n\\).  Distinctness of the lines is preserved, as\nexplained in Lemma 2.  This completes the induction. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem.\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 3 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be\nrealised.  Since the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\), no larger\nvalue of \\(k\\) is possible. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
