ese are non‑sunny and distinct from each other and from the sunny lines \\(S_i\\) (the latter are not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nConsequently we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\nIt remains to prove that every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and set \\(s=a+b\\).  \nBecause \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) there are two possibilities.\n\n*Case 1: \\(s\\le n-k+1\\).*  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the diagonal \\(D_s\\) from (8), so it is covered.\n\n*Case 2: \\(s\\ge n-k+2\\).*  \nSince \\(s\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le k\\) then the sunny line \\(S_a\\) from (7) contains the point \\((a,1)\\) and, because it has a well‑defined (non‑vertical) slope, it contains **all** points with abscissa \\(a\\); in particular it contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n\nIf \\(a>k\\) then \\(s=a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(k+1)=n-k\\).  \nHence \\(b\\le n-k\\) and consequently \\(s=a+b\\le a+(n-k)\\le (n)+(n-k)=2n-k\\).  \nBut \\(s\\ge n-k+2\\) forces \\(a\\le k\\), contradicting \\(a>k\\).  \nThus the situation \\(a>k\\) cannot occur in this case, and we must have \\(a\\le k\\), which reduces to the previous sub‑case.\n\nTherefore every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable.  \nConversely, the number of sunny lines cannot be negative nor exceed the total number of lines \\(n\\).  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are all }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or j