            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (All‑diagonal covering)\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x+y=i+1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), and  \n\n\\[\nS\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(x+y=a+b\\), which is one of the \\(L_i\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Choosing points for sunny lines)\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct points\n\\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\in S\\) such that no two of them lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake \\(P_j=(j,1)\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \nIf two of these points were on a line of slope \\(-1\\) we would have \\(j_1+1=j_2+1\\), i.e. \\(j_1=j_2\\), contradicting distinctness. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nUse the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(L_i\\) of Lemma 1. All lines are non‑sunny, so the required number of sunny lines is \\(0\\).\n\n*Case \\(k\\ge 1\\).*  \n\n1. By Lemma 2 choose distinct points \\(P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\in S\\) with no two sharing slope \\(-1\\).  \n2. Through each \\(P_j\\) draw a line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\;y- b_j = m\\,(x-a_j),\\qquad m\\neq 0,\\;m\\neq\\infty,\\;m\\neq -1,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\((a_j,b_j)=P_j\\).  \n   All \\(S_j\\) are sunny and contain the point \\(P_j\\).  \n\n3. Let  \n\n   \\[\n   T=S\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T\\) the sum \\(c=a+b\\) belongs to  \n\n   \\[\n   C:=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{a_1+b_1,\\dots ,a_k+b_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(|C|\\le n-k\\).  \n\n4. For each \\(c\\in C\\) add the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_c:\\;x+y=c .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are non‑sunny and together contain all points of \\(T\\).\n\nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\nk+|C|\\le k+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nIf fewer than \\(n\\) lines have been obtained, insert any additional lines of the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)); such lines are non‑sunny, remain distinct from the already constructed ones, and do not affect the covering property. After this step we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(S\\) is either one of the chosen \\(P_j\\) (hence lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\)) or belongs to \\(T\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\). Thus each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); all other lines are of the prohibited directions and are therefore non‑sunny. Consequently the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both conditions, and clearly the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, which is \\(n\\). Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
