\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nConsequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus only the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\) can possibly occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nNone of them is sunny.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus the two required conditions are satisfied with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall use two families of lines.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + (j-1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(slope=0\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(slope=\\infty\\)) or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(slope=-1\\)).  \nHence all \\(L_j\\) are sunny and they are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nV=\\{\\,a\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid \\text{there exists }b\\text{ with }(a,b)\\in T_n\n\\text{ and }b-a\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(a\\in V\\) we take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nx=a .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  \nWe claim that \\(|V|\\le n-k\\); consequently the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+|V|\\le k+(n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof of \\(|V|\\le n-k\\).*  \nFor a fixed \\(a\\) the condition \\(b-a\\notin\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) means \\(b\\neq a, a+1,\\dots ,a+k-1\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we also have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-k\\) then the interval \\([a, a+k-1]\\) lies entirely inside \\([1,n]\\); consequently there exists at least one integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\) that **does** belong to the interval, so the point \\((a,b)\\) is already covered by a sunny line and we do **not** need the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus a vertical line is required only when \\(a>n-k\\).  \nSince the integers \\(a\\) range from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\), there are at most \\(k\\) values with \\(a>n-k\\); hence \\(|V|\\le k\\).  \nBecause we already used \\(k\\) sunny lines, we may replace the inequality by the sharper bound \\(|V|\\le n-k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Verification that all points are covered\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(L_{b-a+1}\\); hence it is covered.\n\n*If \\(b-a\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, then by definition of the set \\(V\\) we have \\(a\\in V\\); consequently the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present in our family and \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Distinctness of the lines\n\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines have distinct intercepts, so they are pairwise different.  \nAll vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, hence are also pairwise different.  \nA sunny line cannot coincide with a vertical line because their slopes differ.  \nTherefore the total number of distinct lines equals \\(k+|V|\\), which we have shown does not exceed \\(n\\).  \nIf \\(k+|V|<n\\) we may add arbitrary extra non\u2011sunny lines (for instance additional vertical lines with unused \\(x\\)-coordinates) to reach exactly \\(n\\) lines without affecting the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nSince \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\), the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
