"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThat is, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions, and no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Trivial upper bound.**  Because we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\); hence \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non\u2011sunny and together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n3. **General construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n   *Take \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\):*  \n   \\[\n   L_j:\\; y = x + (j-1),\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,k .\n   \\]\n   *Take the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines:*  \n   \\[\n   V_i:\\; x = i ,\\qquad i = 1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n   \\]\n   The sunny lines cover all points whose difference \\(b-a\\) lies in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\); the vertical lines cover the rest.  \n\n4. **Verification.**  For any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) either \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) (so the point lies on some \\(L_j\\)) or \\(a\\le n-k\\) (so the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)). Thus every required point is covered.\n\n5. **Distinctness and exact number of lines.**  The \\(k\\) sunny lines have distinct intercepts, the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, and a sunny line cannot coincide with a vertical line. Hence we have exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, with precisely \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\nSince we have exhibited a valid configuration for every \\(k\\) in the range \\([0,n]\\) and no larger \\(k\\) is possible, the answer is the whole set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); equivalently it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nConsequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus only the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\) can possibly occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\]\n\nNone of them is sunny.  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nHence the two required conditions are satisfied with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall use two families of lines.\n\n#### 3.1 Sunny lines  \n\nFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + (j-1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(slope=0\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(slope=\\infty\\)) or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(slope=-1\\)).  \nThus all \\(L_j\\) are sunny, and they are distinct because they have different \\(y\\)-intercepts.\n\n#### 3.2 Vertical lines  \n\nFor every integer  \n\n\\[\ni\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\n\\]\n\ntake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\; x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, then \\(b = a + (j-1)\\) for some \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(L_j\\) and is covered.\n\n*If \\(b-a\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, then \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nIndeed, from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  \nIf \\(a>n-k\\) then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and therefore  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k,\n\\]\n\nwhich forces \\(b-a\\le k-1\\), contradicting the assumption.  \nHence \\(a\\le n-k\\), and the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which exists because \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\)) contains the point \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Distinctness and exact number of lines\n\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) have distinct intercepts, so they are pairwise different.  \nThe vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, so they are also pairwise different.  \nA sunny line cannot coincide with a vertical line because their slopes differ.  \n\nHence the total number of distinct lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-k)=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nSince \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\), the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
