1cUse the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines as **vertical** lines  \n> \\(V_i:\\; x = i ,\\; i = 1,2,\\dots ,n-k.\\)  \n> In addition, for those points that are not covered by the vertical lines we introduce **horizontal** lines.  \n> Specifically, for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define  \n> \\(H_t:\\; y = t.\\)  \n> We will keep only the first \\(n-k\\) of these horizontal lines; i.e. we take \\(H_t\\;(t=1,\\dots ,n-k).\\)\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Up to this point the description is clear: we have \\(k\\) sunny lines, \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, and also \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines. However, the total number of lines is now \\(k + (n-k) + (n-k) = n + (n-k) > n\\) (unless \\(k=n\\)). The solution acknowledges this excess and attempts to \u201cmerge\u201d lines in the next step.\n\n#### 4. \u201cMerging\u201d vertical and horizontal lines\n> \u201cTo obtain exactly \\(n\\) lines we **merge** each vertical line \\(V_i\\) with the corresponding horizontal line \\(H_i\\) by replacing the pair \\(\\{V_i,H_i\\}\\) with a single line  \n> \\(L_i:\\; x = i \\quad\\text{or}\\quad y = i ,\\)  \n> whichever of the two is needed for the covering argument (see step\u202f3).  \n> Consequently we end up with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines: the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_j\\) and the \\(n-k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) (each of which is either vertical or horizontal, hence non\u2011sunny).\u201d\n\n**Issue Identified (Critical Error):**  \nThe \u201cmerge\u201d operation is not justified. Replacing a pair \\(\\{V_i, H_i\\}\\) by a single line discards one of the two directions. If we keep only the vertical line \\(x=i\\), any point that was covered solely by the horizontal line \\(y=i\\) (i.e. points with \\(y=i\\) but \\(x\\neq i\\)) may become uncovered, and vice\u2011versa. The solution provides no argument that for each index \\(i\\) the retained orientation still covers all points that required the discarded orientation. Consequently the claim that after merging we still cover **all** lattice points is unsupported.\n\nBecause the subsequent verification (step\u202f5) relies on the existence of the vertical lines \\(V_a\\) for certain values of \\(a\\), the failure of the merging step undermines the entire construction.\n\n#### 5. Verification of coverage \u2013 case analysis\nThe solution splits the points into two cases.\n\n##### Case\u202f1: \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)\n> \u201cThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\u201d\n\n**Assessment:** Correct. If \\(b-a=j-1\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\), the point satisfies \\(y = x + (j-1)\\) and is on \\(S_j\\).\n\n##### Case\u202f2: \\(b-a\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) (i.e. \\(b-a<0\\) or \\(b-a\\ge k\\))\n\n**Sub\u2011case 2a: \\(b-a<0\\) (so \\(b<a\\)).**  \nThe solution writes:\n\n> \u201cFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> a \\le \\frac{n+1+b}{2} \\le \\frac{n+1+(a-1)}{2},\n> \\]  \n> whence \\(a\\le n-k\\). Consequently the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which is one of the lines \\(L_i\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\u201d\n\n**Issue Identified (Critical Error):**  \n\n1. The inequality \\(a \\le \\frac{n+1+b}{2}\\) follows from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) by adding \\(a\\) to both sides and dividing by 2, which is valid.  \n\n2. The next step claims \\(\\frac{n+1+b}{2} \\le \\frac{n+1+(a-1)}{2}\\). Since \\(b<a\\), we have \\(b\\le a-1\\); substituting gives \\(\\frac{n+1+b}{2} \\le \\frac{n+1+(a-1)}{2}\\). **This inequality is correct** (the direction is preserved because we are adding the smaller number \\(b\\) on the left).  \n\n   However, from the two inequalities we only deduce  \n   \\[\n   a \\le \\frac{n+1+(a-1)}{2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 2a \\le n + a \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a \\le n .\n   \\]\n   The conclusion is merely \\(a\\le n\\), **not** \\(a\\le n-k\\). The extra subtraction of \\(k\\) is unjustified.  \n\n3. Even if we had \\(a\\le n\\), the construction provides vertical lines only for indices \\(1,\\dots ,n-k\\). For many choices of \\(k\\) (e.g. \\(k\\)