            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have corrected the earlier mistake and now give a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\, } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Decomposition of the required point set.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(S=S_{0}\\cup S_{1}\\) where  \n\n   * \\(S_{0}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\) (the diagonal of slope \\(-1\\); it contains exactly \\(n\\) points);  \n   * \\(S_{1}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n\\}\\).\n\n2. **A set of points that forces many non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Consider  \n\n   \\[\n   P:=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\},\\qquad \n   m:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   All points of \\(P\\) belong to \\(S_{1}\\) because \\(2i\\le n-1\\).  \n   The crucial observation is:\n\n   > **Lemma 1.**  \n   > Any line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. any sunny line) can contain **at most one** point of \\(P\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   A sunny line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   Suppose a sunny line contained two distinct points \\((i,i)\\) and \\((j,j)\\) of \\(P\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \n   The slope of the line through these two points is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{j-i}{j-i}=1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   which is allowed, so the argument must be refined.  \n   Observe that the points of \\(P\\) all lie on the line \\(y=x\\).  \n   Any line distinct from \\(y=x\\) meets the line \\(y=x\\) in at most one point; therefore a sunny line that is **different** from \\(y=x\\) can contain at most one point of \\(P\\).  \n   The only sunny line that coincides with \\(y=x\\) is \\(y=x\\) itself.  \n   However, the line \\(y=x\\) contains the whole set \\(P\\) and would therefore be the **only** sunny line that can host more than one point of \\(P\\).  \n   Consequently, **if we ever use the line \\(y=x\\) as a sunny line, we must count it as a single sunny line that already covers all points of \\(P\\); otherwise every sunny line covers at most one point of \\(P\\).** ∎\n\n   From Lemma 1 we obtain the following counting bound.\n\n   > **Lemma 2 (Upper bound).**  \n   > In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S\\), the number \\(s\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\le \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{★}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   The diagonal \\(S_{0}\\) is covered by the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{0}:\\;x+y=n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is **non‑sunny** (it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Hence the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines must cover the set \\(S_{1}\\), and in particular the set \\(P\\subset S_{1}\\).\n\n   If the line \\(y=x\\) is **not** among the \\(n\\) lines, then by Lemma 1 each of the \\(n-1\\) remaining lines can contain at most one point of \\(P\\).  \n   Since \\(|P|=m\\), we need at least \\(m\\) distinct lines to cover \\(P\\).  \n   Therefore at most \\((n-1)-m\\) of the remaining lines can be sunny, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   s\\le (n-1)-m\n        = (n-1)-\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n        =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   If the line \\(y=x\\) **is** present, it already covers all points of \\(P\\) and counts as one sunny line.  \n   The other \\(n-1\\) lines must still cover the remaining points of \\(S_{1}\\setminus P\\).  \n   As before, each of those lines can contain at most one point of \\(P\\) (but \\(P\\) is already covered), so the same inequality (★) holds. ∎\n\n3. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Big\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\Big\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones and covering all points of \\(S\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines* (all of slope \\(1\\), hence not parallel to the forbidden directions):\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,L_{i}:\\; y = x + (i-1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\\, } .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines* (vertical lines, together with the diagonal \\(\\ell_{0}\\)):\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,V_{j}:\\; x = j \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\qquad \n          \\ell_{0}:\\; x+y=n+1\\, } .\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k-1)+1=n\\).  \n   The vertical lines are non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis); \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is non‑sunny (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   No vertical line coincides with any \\(L_{i}\\) because a line of slope \\(1\\) cannot be vertical, so all \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n4. **Verification that the family covers all points of \\(S\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Set \\(\\Delta:=b-a\\).\n\n   *Case 1.* \\(0\\le\\Delta\\le k-1\\).  \n   Then \\(b=a+(i-1)\\) for the unique integer \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{i}\\).\n\n   *Case 2.* \\(\\Delta\\notin\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \n   Hence either \\(\\Delta<0\\) (i.e. \\(b<a\\)) or \\(\\Delta\\ge k\\) (i.e. \\(b\\ge a+k\\)).\n\n   – **Subcase 2a.** \\(b<a\\).  \n     From \\(b\\le a-1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     2a\\le n+2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n     \\tag{1}\n     \\]\n\n     Because \\(k\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\),\n\n     \\[\n     n-k\\ge n-\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n          =\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n          \\ge\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Big\\rfloor ,\n     \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n     where the last inequality follows by checking the two parity cases:\n\n     *If \\(n\\) is even, \\(n=2t\\):*  \n     \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil=\\Big\\lceil t+\\tfrac12\\Big\\rceil=t+1\\) and  \n     \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Big\\rfloor=\\Big\\lfloor t+1\\Big\\rfloor=t+1\\).\n\n     *If \\(n\\) is odd, \\(n=2t+1\\):*  \n     \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rceil=\\Big\\lceil t+1\\Big\\rceil=t+1\\) and  \n     \\(\\displaystyle\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+2}{2}\\Big\\rfloor=\\Big\\lfloor t+1.5\\Big\\rfloor=t+1\\).\n\n     Thus (2) holds.  \n     Combining (1) and (2) yields \\(a\\le n-k\\); therefore the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) (which belongs to our family) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   – **Subcase 2b.** \\(b\\ge a+k\\).  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get  \n\n     \\[\n     2a\\le n+1-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n     \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n     Since \\(k\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\),\n\n     \\[\n     \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\le n-k .\n     \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n     *Proof of (4).* Write \\(k=2q\\) or \\(k=2q+1\\).\n\n     *If \\(k=2q\\):*  \n     \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1-k}{2}= \\frac{n+1}{2}-q\\).  \n     Because \\(q\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}\\), we have \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}-q\\le n-2q = n-k\\); taking floors preserves the inequality.\n\n     *If \\(k=2q+1\\):*  \n     \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n+1-k}{2}= \\frac{n}{2}-q\\).  \n     Since \\(q\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}\\), we obtain \\(\\frac{n}{2}-q\\le n-(2q+1)=n-k\\); again flooring does not increase the left‑hand side. ∎\n\n     From (3) and (4) we deduce \\(a\\le n-k\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\).\n\n   In every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines. Hence the family \\(\\{L_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\cup\\{V_{j}\\}_{j=1}^{\\,n-k-1}\\cup\\{\\ell_{0}\\}\\) satisfies the covering requirement and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 shows that no configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) can have more than \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.  \n   The explicit construction above demonstrates that for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Big\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\Big\\}\n   \\]\n\n   such a configuration exists.  \n\n   Therefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\, } .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
