ines.\n\n*If \\(a\\le m\\):* the point belongs to the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*If \\(a>m\\):* then \\(a\\ge m+1\\).  \nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1=m+k+1\\), whence  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = m+k+1-a = k+1-(a-m).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a-m\\ge1\\), we obtain \\(b\\le k-(a-m)+1\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\na-b\\ge a-(k-(a-m)+1)=2a-m-k-1\\ge m+1 .\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand \\(a-b\\le n-1\\).  \nThus the integer \\(c:=a-b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \nChoosing \\(j=c+1\\) we have \\(c=j-1\\), and the defining equation of \\(S_j\\),\n\n\\[\ny = x-(j-1),\n\\]\n\nis satisfied by \\((a,b)\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_j\\).\n\nTherefore every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.  \nThe lines are distinct: the vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-intercepts, and the sunny\nlines have different intercepts \\(-(j-1)\\).  \nExactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe construction shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\nThe argument of §1 proves that no family can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.\nCombining the two parts we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nas the complete set of admissible values.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
