s are distinct, so the obtained lines are all different.  They are non\u2011sunny and together with the lines from Steps\u202f1 and\u202f2 cover the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n   \\[\n   2\\;(\\text{vertical and horizontal})+k\\;(\\text{sunny})+(n-2-k)\\;(\\text{slope }-1)=n,\n   \\]\n   and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\nCombining the upper bound with the construction gives the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN=|T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition.\nDenote by \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines and by \\(t=n-s\\) the number of\nnon\u2011sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1.1  \nA line of the form (1) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 1\\).  The points of \\(T_n\\) on it are\n\\((c,b)\\) where \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such integers \\(b\\).\nThe same bound holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) by symmetry.\nFor a line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\), the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n\\(c\\le n+1\\).  The integer solutions of \\(a+b=c\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) are\n\\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); their number is \\(c-1\\le n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 1.2  \nA sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the line have equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\nFor each integer \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) there is at most one integer\n\\(y\\) such that \\((x,y)\\) lies on the line, namely \\(y=mx+b\\).  Hence at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on the line.  If the line contained \\(n\\) points,\nthen it would meet the vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=n\\) at integer points.\nThe two points would be \\((1,\\,m+b)\\) and \\((n,\\,mn+b)\\).  Their sum of\ncoordinates equals \\(1+m+b\\) and \\(n+mn+b\\) respectively.  Subtracting gives\n\\((n-1)+(n-1)m\\), which cannot be zero because \\(m\\neq -1\\).  Hence the two\npoints cannot both satisfy the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently at most\n\\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n\n\nNow count the total number of covered points.  The \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines can\ncover at most \\(tn\\) points by Lemma\u202f1.1, and the \\(s\\) sunny lines at most\n\\(s(n-1)\\) points by Lemma\u202f1.2.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nN\\le tn+s(n-1)= (n-s)n+s(n-1)=n^{2}-s .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(N=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-s\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad s\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{s\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Realising every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) such that \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  We construct \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1 Two fixed non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_{V}: x=1 ,\\qquad L_{H}: y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are of the forms (1), hence non\u2011sunny.  They cover all points of \\(T_n\\)\nlying on the first row or first column, i.e. the points \\((1,b)\\) and\n\\((a,1)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nU_{0}=T_n\\setminus\\bigl(L_{V}\\cup L_{H}\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points still uncovered after these two lines.  One checks that  \n\n\\[\n|U_{0}|=N-(2n-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(2n-1)=\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2 Adding \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{i}{k+1}\\qquad\\bigl(0<m_i<1\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), any line of slope \\(m_i\\) is sunny.\n\nWe construct the lines inductively.  \nAssume that after having drawn \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{i-1}\\) the set of uncovered\npoints is \\(U_{i-1}\\neq\\varnothing\\).  Choose the lexicographically smallest\n\\((a_i,b_i)\\in U_{i-1}\\) (smallest \\(a\\), and for that \\(a\\) smallest \\(b\\)).\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=m_i x + c_i,\\qquad\\text{where }c_i=b_i-m_i a_i .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_i\\) passes through \\((a_i,b_i)\\).  The slopes \\(m_i\\) are all distinct,\nso the lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct.  Each \\(L_i\\) is sunny by construction.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nU_i = U_{i-1}\\setminus\\bigl(L_i\\cap T_n\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points still uncovered after the \\(i\\)-th sunny line.\nBecause a sunny line contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma\u202f1.2),\nwe have  \n\n\\[\n|U_i|\\ge |U_{i-1}|-(n-1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nApplying (4) repeatedly and using (3),\n\n\\[\n|U_k|\\ge \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}-k(n-1)\n      = (n-2-k)\\frac{(n-1)}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, each newly added line certainly covers at least one new\npoint, namely \\((a_i,b_i)\\).  Hence after \\(k\\) steps we have removed at least\n\\(k\\) points from \\(U_{0}\\), giving the opposite inequality  \n\n\\[\n|U_k|\\le |U_{0}|-k = \\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}-k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\), the right\u2011hand sides of (5) and (6) coincide and equal\n\\(n-2-k\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|U_k| = n-2-k .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, if \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in U_k\\) with \\((a,b)\\neq(a',b')\\) then\n\\(a+b\\neq a'+b'\\); otherwise the two points would lie on the same line\n\\(x+y=c\\) of slope \\(-1\\), and the earlier construction would have covered the\nlexicographically smaller of them when that line was drawn.  Hence the sums\n\\(a+b\\) are pairwise distinct for the points of \\(U_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3 Covering the remaining points with lines of slope \\(-1\\)\n\nFor each \\((a,b)\\in U_k\\) draw the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{a,b}:\\; x+y = a+b .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(-1\\), so they are non\u2011sunny.  Because the constants\n\\(a+b\\) are distinct, the lines \\(L_{a,b}\\) are distinct.  Each such line\ncontains its defining point \\((a,b)\\); any other point of \\(T_n\\) lying on the\nsame line would already belong to \\(U_k\\) and would have the same sum, which is\nimpossible.  Hence after adding these \\(n-2-k\\) lines every point of \\(T_n\\) is\ncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4 Counting the lines\n\nWe have produced  \n\n* 2 non\u2011sunny lines (\\(L_V, L_H\\));\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\));\n* \\(n-2-k\\) further non\u2011sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2 + k + (n-2-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Thus for the chosen \\(k\\) a suitable\nconfiguration exists.\n\nSince the argument works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), all\nthese values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe universal bound (2) shows that no configuration can have more than\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  The construction of \u00a72 demonstrates that every integer\n\\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n-2\\) inclusive can be achieved.  Therefore the set\nof admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
