            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) one can construct \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny; no other value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Necessary bound.**  \n   A family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines, so any feasible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n2. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *We use three types of lines*  \n\n   - **Vertical lines** \\(V_j:\\;x=j\\)  \\((j=1,\\dots ,n-k-1)\\) – non‑sunny.  \n   - **Diagonal lines** \\(D_t:\\;x+y=t\\) \\((t=n-k,\\dots ,n)\\) – non‑sunny (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   - **Sunny lines** \\(S_i:\\;x-y = i\\) \\((i=1,\\dots ,k)\\) – slope \\(1\\), hence sunny.\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k-1)+(k+1)=n .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) falls into one of three mutually exclusive cases, and in each