            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition contains at most one non‑sunny line.  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) of the lines are sunny; denote the (possible) unique non‑sunny line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\nBecause a sunny line has a slope different from \\(\\infty\\), it meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point.  \nConsequently a sunny line can intersect each of the \\(n\\) vertical columns \\(\\{x=1\\},\\dots ,\\{x=n\\}\\) in at most one point.\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is vertical, say \\(\\ell:x=1\\), then \\(\\ell\\) covers exactly the \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(1\\).  \nAll remaining points of \\(T_n\\) lie in the columns \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\); there are  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n\\]\n\nsuch points.  \nEach of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can meet each of the \\(n-1\\) columns \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) in at most one point, so together they can cover at most \\((n-1)^2\\) of those points.  \nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2>(n-1)^2,\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  \n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\), rotate the picture by \\(90^{\\circ}\\) or reflect in the line \\(y=x\\); the same counting argument applies.  \n\nThus at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge2).\n\\]\n\n**Non‑sunny lines.**  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,m\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese are vertical, hence non‑sunny.\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;y = x-(m+j-1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all \\(S_j\\) are sunny.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines are distinct because the vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-intercepts and the sunny lines have distinct \\(y\\)-intercepts.\n\nWe prove that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le m\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a>m\\).  \nWrite \\(a=m+t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) (the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1=m+k\\) forces \\(t\\le k\\)).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = k+1-t .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\ge1\\), the integer  \n\n\\[\nj:=t+b\\quad\\text{satisfies}\\quad 1\\le j\\le k .\n\\]\n\nNow compute the right‑hand side of (2) for \\(x=a\\) and this \\(j\\):\n\n\\[\nx-(m+j-1)=a-(m+t+b-1)= (m+t)-(m+t+b-1)=b .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_j\\); i.e. \\((a,b)\\in S_j\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered either by a vertical line (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\)) or by one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate exceeds \\(m\\)).  \n\nThe construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k=m\\) non‑sunny lines, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nThe upper bound (1) together with the explicit construction shows that the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
