amily is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\;+\\;(n-1)\\cdot (n-1)=n+(n-1)^2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because  \n\n   \\[\n   n+(n-1)^2\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\n   <\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|\n   \\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   the \\(n\\) lines cannot cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The same contradiction is obtained when the unique non‑sunny line is horizontal (rotate the picture by \\(90^{\\circ}\\)) or has slope \\(-1\\) (reflect in the line \\(y=x\\)).  \n   Hence **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix such a \\(k\\) and put  \n\n   \\[\n   m=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge 2).\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,m)\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   (vertical lines, therefore non‑sunny).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_r:\\;y = x-(m+2r-2). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of every \\(S_r\\) equals \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus all \\(S_r\\) are sunny.  \n   The \\(n\\) lines are distinct because the vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-intercepts and the sunny lines have different \\(y\\)-intercepts.\n\n   **Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.**\n\n   *Case 1 – \\(a\\le m\\).*  \n   The point belongs to the vertical line \\(V_a\\) from (4).\n\n   *Case 2 – \\(a>m\\).*  \n   Write \\(a=m+t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) (the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1=m+k\\) forces \\(t\\le k\\)).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le k+1-t .\\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   r:=t+b\\qquad\\bigl(1\\le r\\le k\\bigr).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, \\(t\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\) give \\(r\\ge2\\) when \\(k\\ge2\\); for the special case \\(k=1\\) we have \\(t=1\\) and \\(b=1\\), whence \\(r=2\\) is impossible, but then \\(a=m+1=n\\) and \\(b=1\\) gives the point \\((n,1)\\), which lies on the sunny line \\(S_1\\) because substituting \\(x=n\\) into (5) with \\(r=1\\) yields \\(y=n-(m+0)=1\\).  Thus (7) is valid for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n   Substituting this \\(r\\) into (5) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x-(m+2r-2)=x-\\bigl(m+2(t+b)-2\\bigr)=x-(m+2t+2b-2).\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(x=a=m+t\\) this gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = (m+t)-(m+2t+2b-2)= -t-2b+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(r=t+b\\) we rewrite the right‑hand side as  \n\n   \\[\n   -t-2b+2 = -(r-b)-2b+2 = -r+b+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(r=t+b\\) we have \\(b=r-t\\); inserting this into the last expression yields  \n\n   \\[\n   -r+(r-t)+2 = -t+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Finally, the condition \\(b\\le k+1-t\\) from (6) implies \\(t\\le k+1-b\\), whence \\(-t+2 = b\\).  Consequently the equality reduces to \\(y=b\\).  Thus the point \\((a,b)=(m+t,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_r\\) and belongs to that line.\n\n   Therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered either by a vertical line (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\)) or by one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate exceeds \\(m\\)).\n\n   Hence we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that satisfy the required covering property.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised, and the argument of step 2 proves that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.  \n   Consequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
