  Compute the quantity  \n\n   \\[\n   c:=a-b = (m+t)-b .\n   \\]\n\n   Using (7) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   m+t-(k+1-t)\\le c\\le m+t-1,\n   \\]\n   i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   m\\le c\\le m+k-1 .\\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(c\\) can be written uniquely as \\(c=m+r-1\\) with an integer \\(r\\) satisfying \\(1\\le r\\le k\\).  \n   Substituting \\(c=m+r-1\\) into the equation of the sunny line \\(S_r\\) (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x-(m+r-1) .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(x=a\\) this yields \\(y = a-(m+r-1)=c-(m+r-1)=b\\).  \n   Thus the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_r\\) and lies on that line.\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered either by a vertical line (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\)) or by one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines (if its \\(x\\)-coordinate exceeds \\(m\\)).\n\n   Therefore we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that satisfy the required covering property.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised, and the argument of step 2 proves that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.  \n   Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
