            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition “for all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines’’ is exactly that the family of lines covers the finite set \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the number of integer solutions of \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\); this is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is of one of the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }y=c,\\qquad\\text{vertical }x=c,\\qquad\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=c).\n\\]\nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Lemma 1 – a sunny line meets \\(S_n\\) in at most two points  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nSuppose, for a contradiction, that \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points of \\(S_n\\),\n\\[\nP_i=(a_i,b_i)\\qquad(i=1,2,3),\n\\]\nwith \\(a_i,b_i\\ge 1\\) and \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\).\n\nBecause the three points are collinear, the slopes between any two of them are equal:\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}= \\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}=m .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf two of the \\(a_i\\) were equal, say \\(a_1=a_2\\), then the left‑hand side of (2) would be infinite, forcing the line to be vertical, contradicting that \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \nThus the three \\(a_i\\) are pairwise distinct; the same argument shows the three \\(b_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nConsequently the three sums \\(s_i=a_i+b_i\\) are also distinct.  \nBut the set \\(\\{s_i\\}\\) is a subset of \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), which contains exactly \\(n\\) elements.  \nHence three distinct points of \\(S_n\\) can be collinear **only** when the line has one of the forbidden slopes:\n* if the three points share the same \\(a\\)-coordinate, the line is vertical;\n* if they share the same \\(b\\)-coordinate, the line is horizontal;\n* if they share the same sum \\(a+b\\), the line has slope \\(-1\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, none of these three possibilities can occur.  Therefore a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(S_n\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Lemma 2 – an upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(S_n\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nWrite \\(\\mathcal L=\\mathcal S\\cup\\mathcal N\\) where \\(\\mathcal S\\) (resp. \\(\\mathcal N\\)) is the set of sunny (resp. non‑sunny) lines; \\(|\\mathcal S|=k\\) and \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-k\\).\n\n*By Lemma 1* each sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\); hence the total number of points covered by the sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line can be of three types:\n\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); thus it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\);\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) is analogous;\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) and meets the points \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c-a\\le n+1-a\\); a short check shows that such a line also contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nTherefore **every** non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Consequently the points covered by all non‑sunny lines number at most \\(n\\,(n-k)\\).\n\nAltogether we have the inequality  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\;\\le\\;2k\\;+\\;n\\,(n-k). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert the exact value (1) of \\(|S_n|\\) and simplify (3):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le 2k + n^2 - nk\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - n^2 \\le (2-n)k\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n-\\frac{n(n-1)}2 \\le (2-n)k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(2-n<0\\); dividing by the negative number reverses the inequality:\n\n\\[\nk \\le \\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} = n-2+\\frac{2}{n-2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, the right‑hand side forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus **no** configuration can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Lemma 3 – a covering with no sunny line  \n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x_{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}\\,\\}\\cup\n            \\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y_{\\lceil n/2\\rceil}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+\\lceil n/2\\rceil=n\\) lines, all of which are vertical or horizontal, hence non‑sunny.\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); otherwise \\(b\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  In either case the point is covered.  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is a covering of \\(S_n\\) that uses **no** sunny line, i.e. \\(k=0\\) is realizable. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Construction for an arbitrary