+1\\), the only points that could be lost when \\(x=i\\) is removed are those with \\(b\\ge 2\\).  Those points are all covered by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) after the replacement, as shown in the detailed verification below.  Consequently the new family still covers all of \\(S_n\\) and now contains exactly the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 shows that \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are impossible; the construction shows that every integer \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be achieved.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n#### 1.  The set to be covered  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Lemma 1 – maximal number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) in the form \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\) then \\(b=ma+b_0\\) for some constant \\(b_0\\).  \nBecause \\(a,b\\) are positive integers and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na+ma+b_0\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\bigl(1+m\\bigr)\\le n+1-b_0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m\\neq-1\\), the factor \\(1+m\\) is a non‑zero rational number.  \nLet \\(d\\) be the denominator of \\(m\\) when written in lowest terms; then \\(1+m\\) has denominator \\(d\\) as well.  \nInequality (2) shows that the integer variable \\(a\\) is bounded above by a constant divided by \\(|1+m|\\).  Consequently the number of admissible integer values of \\(a\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|1+m|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe expression \\(|1+m|\\) is minimized (over all sunny slopes) when \\(m=1\\), giving \\(|1+m|=2\\).  Hence the **largest possible** number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can lie on a sunny line is  \n\n\\[\ns:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(y=x\\) (slope 1) indeed contains the points \\((1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\bigr)\\), showing that the bound (3) is sharp. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Lemma 2 – upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\).  \nDenote by \\(\\mathcal S\\) the set of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and by \\(\\mathcal N\\) the set of non‑sunny lines; let  \n\n\\[\nk:=|\\mathcal S|,\\qquad |\\mathcal N|=n-k .\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contains at most \\(s\\) points of \\(S_n\\); therefore the total number of points covered by all sunny lines is at most \\(ks\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); thus it contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\) and for a line of slope \\(-1\\) (equation \\(x+y=c\\)).  Consequently each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, and all non‑sunny lines together contribute at most \\(n(n-k)\\) points.\n\nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|\\;\\le\\; ks \\;+\\; n(n-k).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|S_n|\\) from (1) and \\(s=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Because \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(s\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\), and (4) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le k\\frac{n+1}{2}+n(n-k)\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - n^2 \\le \\frac{n+1}{2}k - nk\n\\]\n\\[\n\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n-\\frac{n(n-1)}2 \\le -\\frac{n-1}{2}k\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nk \\le n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus any covering by \\(n\\) lines can contain at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Lemma 3 – a covering with no sunny line  \n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\underbrace{\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}}_{\\text{vertical}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{x+y=n+1\\}}_{\\text{slope }-1}.\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n\n*If \\(a\\le n-1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n*If \\(a=n\\)*, then \\(b\\le 1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); the only possibility is \\((n,1)\\), which satisfies \\(n+1= x+y\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  Consequently \\(k=0\\) is attainable. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nStarting from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) we will replace the first \\(k\\) vertical lines by sunny lines of slope 1.\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) perform the following operation:\n\n*Delete* the vertical line \\(x=i\\).\n\n*Insert* the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-x = 1-i\\qquad\\text{(equivalently }y = x + 1 - i\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\), hence it is sunny.  \nIt passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,2),\n\\]\n\nboth of which belong to \\(S_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\ni+1\\le n-1\\;(i\\le k\\le n-2)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; i+1+1\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\\[\ni+2\\le n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; (i+1)+2\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) still covers all of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**(a) No loss of points.**  \n\nWhen the vertical line \\(x=i\\) is removed, the points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on it are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((i,1)\\) is now covered by \\(\\ell_i\\).  \nFor any \\(b\\ge 2\\) we have  \n\n\\[\ni+b\\le i+(n+1-i)=n+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((i,b)\\) satisfies \\(x+y=n+1\\) **iff** \\(b=n+1-i\\).  \nThus the only point of the deleted vertical line that is *not* on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) is \\((i,1)\\), which is rescued by \\(\\ell_i\\).  Consequently every point formerly on \\(x=i\\) remains covered after the replacement.\n\nAll other lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are untouched, so their covered points are unchanged.\n\n---\n\n**(b) Distinctness of the new lines.**  \n\nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(\\ell_j\\) have different intercepts \\(1-i\\neq 1-j\\); hence they are distinct.  Moreover none of the remaining vertical lines coincides with any \\(\\ell_i\\) because the latter have slope 1 while the former are vertical.\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n---\n\n**(c) Number of sunny lines.**  \n\nThe set \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\) consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines, and all other lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are the unchanged non‑sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\nTherefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  Final conclusion  \n\nLemma 2 shows that a covering by \\(n\\) lines cannot have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 3 provides a covering with \\(k=0\\).  \nThe construction of §5 yields coverings for every intermediate value \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\).  \n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
