es at most \\(n\\) points (a horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line meets at most one point in each of the \\(n\\) rows).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\\le ks+n(n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(s\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) and simplifying gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus no covering by \\(n\\) lines can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3 (a covering with no sunny line).**  \n   The following \\(n\\) lines are all non\u2011sunny and together cover \\(S_n\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) either \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains it, or \\(a=n\\) and then \\(b=1\\), whence \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the diagonal line covers it.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n5. **Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) we replace, one by one, up to \\(n-2\\) of the vertical lines by sunny lines of slope\u202f1 that **share a point with the diagonal**; this guarantees that no point is lost.\n\n   For a chosen integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) perform the replacement  \n\n   *delete* the vertical line \\(x=i\\);  \n\n   *insert* the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y-x = 1-i\\qquad\\bigl(\\text{equivalently }y = x+1-i\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,2),\n   \\]\n\n   both belonging to \\(S_n\\) because \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Moreover \\(\\ell_i\\) also passes through the point  \n\n   \\[\n   R_i=(i,n+1-i),\n   \\]\n\n   which lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\); indeed substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=n+1-i\\), and the three points \\(P_i,R_i,Q_i\\) are collinear because they have the same value of \\(y-x\\).\n\n   Consequently after the replacement the point \\((i,b)\\) with any admissible \\(b\\) is still covered:\n\n   * the top point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) is on the diagonal (still present);\n   * the bottom point \\((i,1)\\) is on the new sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\);\n   * every intermediate point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-i\\) lies on the diagonal as well, because for those \\(b\\) we have \\(i+b\\le n+1\\) and the equality \\(i+b=n+1\\) holds exactly when \\(b=n+1-i\\); all other intermediate points are covered by the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) after we have **kept** that line in the family.\n\n   Repeating the replacement for \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) we obtain the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *All lines are distinct*: the \\(\\ell_i\\) have distinct intercepts \\(1-i\\), and none coincides with a remaining vertical line.  \n   *All points of \\(S_n\\) are covered*: the argument above shows that for each column \\(i\\le k\\) the deleted vertical line is compensated by the diagonal line together with the new sunny line; columns \\(i>k\\) are still covered by their original vertical lines, and the diagonal line itself is unchanged.  \n   *Number of sunny lines*: exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny; the other \\(n-k\\) lines are the unchanged non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f2 shows that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible; Lemma\u202f3 and the construction above show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  Therefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]"

