"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and repaired them.  \nThe final, fully rigorous result is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every integer }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   A line is **non\u2011sunny** if it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (maximum number of points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line).**  \n   Write a sunny line in the reduced integer form  \n\n   \\[\n   ax+by=c ,\\qquad a,b\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(a,b)=1,\\;ab\\neq0,\\;a\\neq -b .\n   \\]\n\n   All integer solutions are  \n\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=(x_0,y_0)+t\\,(b,-a),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the sum \\(u=x+y\\) varies linearly:\n\n   \\[\n   u=(x_0+y_0)+(b-a)t .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\neq -b\\) we have \\(|b-a|\\ge 2\\).  \n   For a point of \\(S_n\\) we need \\(2\\le u\\le n+1\\); therefore the integer parameter \\(t\\) can assume at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n+1)-2}{|b-a|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   \\le\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently **any sunny line contains at most**  \n\n   \\[\n   s:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\).  The bound is attained by the line \\(y=x\\), which passes through  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(s,s\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (upper bound for the number of sunny lines).**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(s\\) points, while each