ough the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1),\\qquad Q_i=(i+1,2),\n   \\]\n\n   both belonging to \\(S_n\\) because \\(i\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Moreover \\(\\ell_i\\) also passes through the point  \n\n   \\[\n   R_i=(i,n+1-i),\n   \\]\n\n   which lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\); indeed substituting \\(x=i\\) gives \\(y=n+1-i\\), and the three points \\(P_i,R_i,Q_i\\) are collinear because they have the same value of \\(y-x\\).\n\n   Consequently after the replacement the column \\(i\\) is still completely covered:\n\n   * the top point \\((i,n+1-i)\\) is on the unchanged diagonal line;\n   * the bottom point \\((i,1)\\) is on the new sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\);\n   * every intermediate point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le n-i\\) also lies on the diagonal, because for those \\(b\\) we have \\(i+b\\le n+1\\) and equality holds exactly when \\(b=n+1-i\\); all other intermediate points satisfy \\(i+b<n+1\\) and are therefore on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) after we have **kept** that line in the family.\n\n   Repeating the replacement for \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) we obtain the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *All lines are distinct*: the \\(\\ell_i\\) have distinct intercepts \\(1-i\\), and none coincides with a remaining vertical line.  \n   *All points of \\(S_n\\) are covered*: the argument above shows that for each column \\(i\\le k\\) the deleted vertical line is compensated by the diagonal line together with the new sunny line; columns \\(i>k\\) are still covered by their original vertical lines, and the diagonal line itself is unchanged.  \n   *Number of sunny lines*: exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny; the other \\(n-k\\) lines are the unchanged non\u2011sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all point