ne \\(y=x\\), which passes through  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(s,s\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (upper bound for the number of sunny lines).**  \n   Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n   By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(s\\) points, while each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points (a horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line meets at most one point in each of the \\(n\\) rows).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\n   \\le ks+n(n-k).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(s\\) from (5) and solving (6) for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus no covering by \\(n\\) lines can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3 (a covering with no sunny line).**  \n   The following \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover \\(S_n\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) either \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) contains it, or \\(a=n\\) and then \\(b=1\\), whence \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the diagonal line covers it.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n5. **Lemma\u202f4 (construction for any admissible \\(k\\)).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Starting from the family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) in (8) we replace, one by one, the first \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\) by the following sunny lines of slope\u202f1:\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y-x = 1-i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=k\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   *Distinctness.*  \n   The lines \\(\\ell_i\\) have distinct intercepts \\(1-i\\); none of them coincides with a remaining vertical line or w