n-2\\), all values  \n\n\\[\nk=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\n\\]\n\nare attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most two points\n\n**Lemma 1.** *If a line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), then it contains at most two points of \\(S\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a line \\(\\ell\\) of slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S\\).  \nBecause all three points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), the differences \\((a_i-a_j,b_i-b_j)\\) have non‑negative coordinates and satisfy \\((a_i-a_j)+(b_i-b_j)\\le n\\).  \n\nIf \\(m\\) were not one of the three forbidden slopes, the three points would have to be collinear with a slope that forces at least one of the differences to have either zero \\(x\\)-component (horizontal), zero \\(y\\)-component (vertical) or \\(b_i-b_j=-(a_i-a_j)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). This contradicts the hypothesis on \\(m\\). Hence such a triple cannot exist, and \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(S\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(S\\).  \nDenote by \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) and by \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Contribution of sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most two points of \\(S\\); therefore the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2s\\) points.\n\n*Contribution of non‑sunny lines.*  \nA non‑sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nTwo distinct lines of the same type are parallel and would cover exactly the same set of points of \\(S\\); consequently at most one line of each type can be useful. Hence at most **two** non‑sunny lines can appear in a minimal covering family (e.g