            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S\\) are precisely the integer lattice points inside the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). A simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence for \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\)\n\n**Base configuration (\\(k=0\\)).**  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\). Thus the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers \\(S\\) and contains no sunny line, i.e. it realises \\(k=0\\).\n\n**Adding sunny lines.**  \nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\) replace the vertical line \\(L_i\\) by  \n\n\\[\nM_i:\\;y=(i-1)x+(n+2-i). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(M_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\); for \\(i\\ge1\\) this slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so each \\(M_i\\) is sunny.  \nIf we set \\(x=i\\) in (3) we obtain \\(y=n+1-i\\); consequently the points  \n\n\\[\n(i,1),\\;(i,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(i,n+1-i\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nwhich were covered by the removed vertical line \\(L_i\\) are still covered by \\(M_i\\).  \n\nThe remaining vertical lines \\(L_{t+1},\\dots ,L_n\\) are left unchanged.  \nHence the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_t=\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_t,\\,L_{t+1},\\dots ,L_n\\} \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(S\\), and contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.  \n\nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\), all values  \n\n\\[\nk=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\n\\]\n\nare attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A sunny line meets \\(S\\) in at most two points\n\n**Lemma 1.** *If a line has slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\), then it contains at most two points of \\(S\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a line \\(\\ell\\) of slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in S\\).  \nBecause all three points satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\), the differences \\((a_i-a_j,b_i-b_j)\\) have non‑negative coordinates and satisfy \\((a_i-a_j)+(b_i-b_j)\\le n\\).  \n\nIf \\(m\\) were not one of the three forbidden slopes, the three points would have to be collinear with a slope that forces at least one of the differences to have either zero \\(x\\)-component (horizontal), zero \\(y\\)-component (vertical) or \\(b_i-b_j=-(a_i-a_j)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)). This contradicts the hypothesis on \\(m\\). Hence such a triple cannot exist, and \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(S\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover all points of \\(S\\).  \nDenote by \\(s\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) and by \\(t=n-s\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Contribution of sunny lines.*  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most two points of \\(S\\); therefore the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2s\\) points.\n\n*Contribution of non‑sunny lines.*  \nA non‑sunny line is either vertical (\\(x=c\\)), horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \nTwo distinct lines of the same type are parallel and would cover exactly the same set of points of \\(S\\); consequently at most one line of each type can be useful. Hence at most **two** non‑sunny lines can appear in a minimal covering family (e.g. one vertical and one horizontal, or one vertical and one slope \\(-1\\), etc.). Thus  \n\n\\[\nt\\le 2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Counting points.*  \nAll points of \\(S\\) must be covered, so using (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le 2s + \\text{(points covered by the }t\\text{ non‑sunny lines)}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S\\); a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains \\(n+1-c\\) points; a line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=c\\) contains at most \\(\\lfloor c/2\\rfloor\\) points, and in any case no non‑sunny line can contain more than \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\). Therefore the total contribution of the non‑sunny lines is at most \\(2n\\). Combining this with (6) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le 2s + 2n. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(s=n-t\\). Substituting into (7) and using \\(t\\ge 0\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le 2(n-t) + 2n = 4n - 2t \\le 4n. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 4n\\) (equivalently \\(n^{2}-7n>0\\)), a contradiction unless \\(t\\ge 2\\). Hence **at least two** non‑sunny lines are necessary; i.e.  \n\n\\[\ns \\le n-2. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus the number of sunny lines satisfies \\(k=s\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of the extreme cases \\(k=n\\) and \\(k=n-1\\)\n\n*Case \\(k=n\\).*  \nThen \\(t=0\\). From Lemma 1 the \\(n\\) sun