\(\\ell : x=c\\) contains at most \\(n-c\\le n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\).*  \nPoints on \\(\\ell\\) are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(c\\le n+1\\).  \nHence the number of points is \\(c-1\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(c-1=n\\) then \\(c=n+1\\); but the point \\((1,n)\\) would satisfy \\(1+n=n+1\\) and belongs to \\(T_n\\), while \\((n,1)\\) also belongs, giving exactly \\(n\\) points on \\(\\ell\\).  \nNevertheless, the total number of points in \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\ge n\\quad (n\\ge 3),\n\\]\n\nso even in the most favourable case the single non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points, leaving at least one point of \\(T_n\\) uncovered because the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are required to pass through that uncovered point, contradicting the hypothesis that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on *some* line.  \n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line cannot exist, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk=n-1\\ \\text{is impossible}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)  \n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n\\]\n\nEach of these lines is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and no line is sunny.  \n\nHence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\)  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because they have different slopes.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  \nSince \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(a\\le n\\).  \nChoose \\(i=a\\) in (3). Then\n\n\\[\nb=\\frac{a}{a+1}\\,a+\\frac{1}{a+1}\n  =\\frac{a^{2}+1}{a+1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to \\(b\\le \\frac{a^{2}+1}{a+1}\\), the equality above holds precisely for the integer \\(b\\) that appears in \\(T_n\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_a\\) and lies on that line.  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\), and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n\nHence \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* The trivial bound \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) holds for any configuration.  \n* The value \\(k=n-1\\) cannot be realized (see (2)).  \n* Configurations exist for \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny) and for \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).\n\nThe status of the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\) remains undetermined.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{At present we know that }k=0\\text{ and }k=n\\text{ are possible, while }k=n-1\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
