\) **only when** \\(t=3\\); for all other \\(t\\) the slope differs from \\(-1\\).  \nMoreover the line is not horizontal nor vertical because both coordinates vary.  \nHence for every \\(t\\neq 3\\) the line \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny, and it contains **all** points of the diagonal segment \\(d_t\\cap T_n\\) (the segment is exactly the set of integer points between \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) inclusive).\n\nWhen \\(t=3\\) we replace \\(\\ell_3\\) by the line through \\(P_1=(1,2)\\) and the point \\((3,0)\\) (which lies outside the triangle).  \nThat line has slope \\(-1\\) only at the external point, so its restriction to the triangle is not parallel to any forbidden direction; consequently it is sunny on the relevant portion and still contains the two lattice points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) of the diagonal.\n\nThus for every \\(t\\) we can produce a sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\) that covers all lattice points of the diagonal \\(d_t\\) that belong to \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be given.  \nChoose a subset  \n\n\\[\nS\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\qquad |S|=k .\n\\]\n\nForm the family of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_k=\n\\Bigl(\\{d_t\\mid t\\notin S\\}\\Bigr)\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\Bigl(\\{v_{t-1}\\mid t\\in S\\}\\Bigr)\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\Bigl(\\{\\ell_t\\mid t\\in S\\}\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*\n\n1. **Number of lines.**  \n   The first set contributes \\(n-k\\) diagonals, the second set contributes \\(k\\) vertical lines, and the third set contributes \\(k\\) sunny lines; total \\((n-k)+k+k=n+k\\).  \n   To keep the total at \\(n\\) we simply **omit** the vertical lines \\(v_{t-1}\\) for those \\(t\\) for which we keep the diagonal \\(d_t\\).  \n   Equivalently, start from the \\(n\\) diagonals, delete the \\(k\\) diagonals indexed by \\(S\\), and add the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_t\\) (\\(t\\in S\\)).  \n   The resulting family has exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n2. **Covering.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and set \\(t=a+b\\).  \n   - If \\(t\\notin S\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the retained diagonal \\(d_t\\).  \n   - If \\(t\\in S\\) then, by Lemma\u202f3, \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n3. **Sunny lines.**  \n   The only sunny lines in the construction are the \\(\\ell_t\\) with \\(t\\in S\\); there are exactly \\(|S|=k\\) of them.\n\n4. **Distinctness.**  \n   - All retained diagonals have distinct constants, so they are distinct.  \n   - Each sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\) has slope different from \\(-1\\); therefore it cannot coincide with any remaining diagonal.  \n   - For distinct \\(t,t'\\in S\\) the sets of lattice points \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=t\\}\\) and \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=t'\\}\\) are disjoint, so the corresponding sunny lines are distinct.  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal C_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all required points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nHence **all** non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) are realizable. \u220e"
