lope \\(-1\\); it is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and hence is **not** sunny.  \n   Since the covering family must consist exactly of the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(\\{d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\\), it contains **no** sunny line.\n\n6. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The family \\(\\{d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\}\\) clearly consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (by definition of the diagonals), and contains zero sunny lines. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n7. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 1\\).**  \n   The argument above shows that any covering with \\(n\\) lines must contain all the diagonals, leaving no room for any additional line of a different slope. Consequently a configuration with at least one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final Answer  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the stated covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
