on of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) must have \\(k=0\\).\n\n7. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{\\,d_t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   clearly consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (by (1)), and contains zero sunny lines.  \n   Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n8. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge 1\\).**  \n   The argument above shows that any covering with exactly \\(n\\) lines must contain all the diagonals, leaving no room for a line whose slope differs from \\(-1\\).  \n   Therefore a configuration with at least one sunny line cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the only non‑negative integer \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]"
