e \\(\\neq-1\\) meets every diagonal in at most one point, the \\(s\\) lines together cover at most \\(s^{2}\\) points of the omitted diagonals, which is enough because \\(P\\le s^{2}\\).  \n   Adding the remaining \\(n-s\\) diagonals (those not in \\(S\\)) gives a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   By construction the \\(s\\) non‑diagonal lines are sunny, so the number of sunny lines equals \\(s\\).\n\n7. **Realisation of every \\(k\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we exhibit a set \\(S\\) of size \\(k\\) satisfying (5).  \n   Take  \n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   Then  \n   \\[\n   P_k=\\sum_{t=2}^{k+1}(t-1)=1+2+\\dots +k=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k^{2}\\qquad(k\\ge 1),\n   \\]\n   and for \\(k=0\\) we have \\(S_0=\\varnothing\\) and \\(P_0=0\\).  \n   Hence (5) holds for every \\(k\\).  \n   Using the construction of step 6 we obtain, for each \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition forces the number of sunny lines to equal the number of omitted diagonals, and the inequality \\(P\\le s^{2}\\) is satisfied for every \\(s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Therefore **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can occur, and no other values are possible because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Final Answer**  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n