uction \\(m=s\\) are correct: we have \\(n\\) total lines, \\(n-s\\) of them are the diagonals we kept, the remaining \\(m\\) are non\u2011diagonal.  \n- The **gap** lies in the identification \\(k=m\\). A *sunny* line is defined as a line **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, **or** the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n  - Any line with slope \\(-1\\) (the diagonals) is **not** sunny, so sunny lines are a subset of the non\u2011diagonal lines.  \n  - However, a non\u2011diagonal line could be horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) or vertical (undefined), which are also **not** sunny.  \n  - The solution assumes implicitly that all non\u2011diagonal lines can be chosen to avoid slopes \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\). This needs justification.  \nThus the equality \\(k=m\\) is not proved; it is a **Justification Gap**. For the remainder of the verification we will **assume** that the construction later indeed makes all non\u2011diagonal lines sunny, so that \\(k=m\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Necessary condition on \\(S\\)\n\n> \u201cThe points that must be covered without using the diagonals in \\(S\\) are \\(P:=\\sum_{t\\in S}(t-1).\\)  \n> By the Lemma, each non\u2011diagonal line can contribute at most one point from each diagonal in \\(S\\); therefore a single non\u2011diagonal line can cover at most \\(s\\) of those points.  \n> Consequently we need at least \\(\\lceil P/s\\rceil\\) non\u2011diagonal lines, i.e. \\(m\\ge\\lceil P/s\\rceil.\\)  \n> Using (2) we obtain \\(s\\ge P/s\\Longrightarrow P\\le s^{2}.\\)\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  \n- The total number of points lying on the omitted diagonals is indeed \\(P\\).  \n- Lemma guarantees at most one point per omitted diagonal per non\u2011diagonal line, giving the capacity bound of \\(s\\) points per line.  \n- Hence at least \\(\\lceil P/s\\rceil\\) such lines are required, and since \\(m=s\\) we obtain the inequality \\(P\\le s^{2}\\). No gaps here.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Sufficiency of the condition  \n\n> \u201cCondition (5) is also sufficient.  \n> Suppose a set \\(S\\subset\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) with \\(|S|=s\\) satisfies \\(P\\le s^{2}\\).  \n> Choose **any** injection that assigns to each diagonal \\(d_t\\) (\\(t\\in S\\)) a distinct point of (1). Because each diagonal contains at least one point, this is possible.  \n> Now construct \\(s\\) non\u2011diagonal lines as follows: for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,s\\) let the \\(i\\)-th line pass through the point chosen from the \\(i\\)-th diagonal of \\(S\\) and through a point of a diagonal not in \\(S\\) (or through a point already covered) with a slope that is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\).  \n> Since a line of slope \\(\\neq-1\\) meets every diagonal in at most one point, the \\(s\\) lines together cover at most \\(s^{2}\\) points of the omitted diagonals, which is enough because \\(P\\le s^{2}\\).  \n> Adding the remaining \\(n-s\\) diagonals (those not in \\(S\\)) gives a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n> By construction the \\(s\\) non\u2011diagonal lines are sunny, so the number of sunny lines equals \\(s\\).\u201d\n\n*Justification Gaps.*\n\n1. **Existence of the injection.**  \n   - The statement \u201cchoose any injection that assigns to each diagonal \\(d_t\\) (\\(t\\in S\\)) a distinct point of (1)\u201d is fine because each diagonal contains \\(t-1\\ge1\\) points, so we can pick one per diagonal. No issue.\n\n2. **Construction of the \\(s\\) lines.**  \n   - The description \u201clet the \\(i\\)-th line pass through the point chosen from the \\(i\\)-th diagonal of \\(S\\) and through a point of a diagonal not in \\(S\\) \u2026 with a slope that is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)\u201d is vague.  \n   - It is not shown that such a second point can always be chosen so that the resulting line has a *new* slope (different from all previously used slopes) and does not coincide with any other constructed line.  \n   - Moreover, it is not proved that the collection of these