 ,y=k_1\\);  \n   - \\(k_2\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=k_2\\);  \n   - one diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   Here \\(k_1+k_2=n-k-1\\) (the \u201c\u20111\u2019\u2019 accounts for the diagonal line).  \n   By a suitable choice of \\(k_1\\) and \\(k_2\\) we can make the union of these lines contain **all** points of \\(S\\) except possibly a small \u201ccorner\u2019\u2019 of size at most \\(2k\\).  \n   Explicitly, set  \n\n   \\[\n   k_1=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\qquad \n   k_2=n-k-1-k_1 .\n   \\]\n\n   One checks that every point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) with either \\(a\\le k_2\\) or \\(b\\le k_1\\) lies on a vertical or horizontal line, respectively, while points with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lie on the diagonal line. The only points not yet covered are those with  \n\n   \\[\n   k_2<a\\le n,\\qquad k_1<b\\le n,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   This set consists of exactly \\(2k\\) points, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   (k_2+1,\\,k_1),\\;(k_2+2,\\,k_1),\\dots ,(n,\\,k_1),\\qquad\n   (k_2,\\,k_1+1),\\;(k_2,\\,k_1+2),\\dots ,(k_2,\\,n).\n   \\]\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 Add the sunny lines.*  \n   For each of the \\(2k\\) uncovered points we draw a distinct sunny line passing through it and through a previously unused point of the grid (for instance, through \\((1,1)\\)). Because the slopes are chosen to avoid \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), each such line is sunny, and all these lines are distinct.  \n\n   Since we have exactly \\(2k\\) uncovered points and we need only \\(k\\) sunny lines, we pair the uncovered points two\u2011by\u2011two: for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) the \\(i\\)-th sunny line is taken to pass through the pair  \n\n   \\[\n   (k_2+i,\\,k_1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (k_2,\\,k_1+i).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of this line is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{k_1-(k_1+i)}{(k_2+i)-k_2}=-\\frac{i}{i}=-1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is **forbidden**. To avoid this, we slightly modify the construction: instead of using the point \\((k_2,\\,k_1+i)\\) we use \\((k_2,\\,k_1+i+1)\\). Then  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{k_1-(k_1+i+1)}{(k_2+i)-k_2}= -\\frac{i+1}{i},\n   \\]\n\n   which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Hence each of the \\(k\\) lines is sunny, distinct, and together with the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines they cover every point of \\(S\\).\n\n4. **Verification.**  \n   - The total number of lines is \\( (n-k) + k = n\\).  \n   - Exactly \\(k\\) of them have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus they are sunny.  \n   - By construction every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.  \n\n   Therefore the required configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\nCombining the impossibility of \\(k=n-1,n\\) with the explicit construction for all smaller \\(k\\) yields the complete solution.\n\n---  \n\n**Answer:** The admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\,}.\n\\]"
