t must be covered.  \nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A convenient covering by non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nConsider the following collection of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\bigl\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{x+y=n+1\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe first two families are horizontal and vertical, hence not sunny; the third line has slope \\(-1\\) and is also not sunny.  \n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies one of the three alternatives:\n\n* if \\(a+b=n+1\\) it lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\);\n* if \\(a+b<n+1\\) then either \\(b\\le n-1\\) (so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)) or \\(a\\le n-1\\) (so it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\)).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) covers \\(T_n\\).  The number of lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_0|= (n-1)+(n-1)+1 = 2n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Replacing a horizontal\u2013vertical pair by a sunny line  \n\nFix integers \\(i,j\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n-1\\).  \nThe two lines \\(x=i\\) and \\(y=j\\) intersect at the point \\((i,j)\\in T_n\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i,j}\\;:\\; y-j = m\\,(x-i)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nbe any line through \\((i,j)\\) whose slope \\(m\\) is **different** from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); such a line is sunny.  \n\nIf we delete the two lines \\(x=i\\) and \\(y=j\\) from a covering family and insert the sunny line \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\), the covering property is preserved because the only point that could have been lost is \\((i,j)\\), and this point now lies on \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\).  No other point of \\(T_n\\) is removed, because all other points were already covered by lines that remain unchanged.\n\nConsequently, each operation of the form  \n\n\\[\n\\{x=i,\\;y=j\\}\\longrightarrow\\{\\ell_{i,j}\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nreduces the total number of lines by one and increases the number of sunny lines by one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(t\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \nChoose any \\(t\\) distinct pairs \\((i_1,j_1),\\dots,(i_t,j_t)\\) with \\(1\\le i_s,j_s\\le n-1\\) and perform the replacement (5) for each of them.  \n\nAfter the \\(t\\) replacements we have\n\n* \\(t\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i_s,j_s}\\);\n* the remaining \\( (n-1)-t\\) horizontal lines and \\((n-1)-t\\) vertical lines;\n* the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHence the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl[(n-1)-t\\bigr]+\\bigl[(n-1)-t\\bigr]+t+1 = n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe number of sunny lines is exactly \\(t\\).  \nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\), we have constructed, for each such \\(t\\), a family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) with precisely \\(k=t\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The case \\(k=n\\)  \n\nTo obtain \\(k=n\\) we start from the configuration with \\(t=n-1\\) sunny lines described above; the only non\u2011sunny line left is the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n\nPick any point \\((p,q)\\in T_n\\) that lies on the diagonal (for instance \\((1,n)\\)).  \nBecause the diagonal is the only line containing \\((p,q)\\), we replace it by a sunny line that also passes through a point already covered by a sunny line, thereby preserving the covering property.  \n\nExplicitly, choose a sunny line through \\((p,q)\\) and any other point of \\(T_n\\) that is already on a sunny line (such a point exists because \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  This new line is sunny, and after deleting the diagonal we again have exactly \\(n\\) lines, all of which are sunny.\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------