on obtained for \\(t=n-1\\) (so we have \\(n-1\\) sunny lines and the diagonal).  \n   The diagonal contains the points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \n   All points \\((i,i)\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) are already covered by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i,i}\\).  \n   The two remaining diagonal points are \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).  \n\n   Replace the diagonal by the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n   Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-n}{2-1}=1-n\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) (since \\(n\\ge3\\)), so \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n   The point \\((2,1)\\) is already covered (it lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{2,1}\\)), therefore the covering of \\(T_n\\) is preserved.  \n   After deleting the diagonal and adding \\(\\ell\\) we still have exactly \\(n\\) lines, now all of them sunny.  \n\n   Consequently a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   By varying the number \\(t\\) of performed replacements from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\) we obtain configurations with exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). No larger value of \\(k\\) can occur because there are only \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A covering by non‑sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\n\\bigl\\{y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{x+y=n+1\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe first two families are horizontal and vertical, hence not sunny; the third line has slope \\(-