ts ,n-1\\}^2\\) and perform the replacement (5) for each pair.  \n\nAfter the \\(t\\) replacements we have  \n\n* \\(t\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i_s,j_s}\\);\n* the untouched horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) for those \\(j\\) not appearing among the chosen \\(j_s\\) (there are \\(n-1-t\\) of them);\n* the untouched vertical lines \\(x=i\\) for those \\(i\\) not appearing among the chosen \\(i_s\\) (again \\(n-1-t\\) of them);\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nConsequently the total number of lines equals  \n\n\\[\n(n-1-t)+(n-1-t)+t+1=n,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nand the number of sunny lines equals \\(t\\).  \nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\), we have constructed, for each such \\(t\\), a family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Obtaining \\(k=n\\)\n\nBegin with the configuration obtained for \\(t=n-1\\); it consists of  \n\n* \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i,i}\\) (one for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\));\n* the diagonal line \\(d:x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThe diagonal \\(d\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n\\]\n\nAll points \\((i,i)\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) are already covered by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i,i}\\).  \nThe two remaining diagonal points are \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).\n\nReplace the diagonal \\(d\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; \\text{through }(1,n)\\text{ and }(2,1).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-n}{2-1}=1-n,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(n\\ge3\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n\nThe point \\((2,1)\\) already lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{2,1}\\); consequently after deleting \\(d\\) and adding \\(\\ell\\) every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered.  \n\nNow the family consists of  \n\n* the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i,i}\\);\n* the new sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\nThus we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny 