            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lie inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A covering with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_0=\\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) are either horizontal or vertical, hence none of them is sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) contains every point \\((a,j)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\); these are exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(j\\).  \nConsequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on some line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).  \n\\(\\mathcal F_0\\) consists of \\((n-1)+1=n\\) distinct lines, so a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A local replacement that creates a sunny line\n\nFix integers \\(i,j\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n-1\\).  \nThe two lines \\(x=i\\) and \\(y=j\\) intersect at the lattice point \\((i,j)\\in T_n\\).  \nChoose any real number \\(m\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq0,\\qquad m\\neq\\infty,\\qquad m\\neq-1,\n\\]\n\nand define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i,j}:\\; y-j=m\\,(x-i).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\) passes through \\((i,j)\\) and, because its slope \\(m\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), it is sunny.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal G\\) be any family of lines that covers \\(T_n\\).  \nIf we delete the two lines \\(x=i\\) and \\(y=j\\) from \\(\\mathcal G\\) and insert \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\), the only point that could have been lost is \\((i,j)\\); this point now belongs to \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\).  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) remain covered, because the remaining lines are unchanged.  \n\nThus the operation  \n\n\\[\n\\{x=i,\\;y=j\\}\\longrightarrow\\{\\ell_{i,j}\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*reduces the total number of lines by one* and *increases the number of sunny lines by one*, while preserving the covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\)\n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) (which has exactly \\(n\\) lines), we will apply the replacement (3) repeatedly.  \nEach application removes two lines and adds one sunny line, decreasing the total count by one.  \nTo restore the count to \\(n\\) we add a new vertical line that has not appeared before; a vertical line is never sunny.  \n\nFormally, after the \\(s\\)-th replacement we add the line \\(x=r_s\\) where the integers \\(r_1,r_2,\\dots\\) are chosen distinct from each other and from \\(1\\).  \nSince vertical lines are non‑sunny, this addition does not affect the number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(t\\) be an integer satisfying \\(0\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \nPerform the following procedure exactly \\(t\\) times:\n\n* choose a pair \\((i_s,j_s)\\) with \\(1\\le i_s,j_s\\le n-1\\) such that the lines \\(x=i_s\\) and \\(y=j_s\\) are still present;\n* apply the replacement (3) to obtain the sunny line \\(\\ell_{i_s,j_s}\\);\n* add a new vertical line \\(x=r_s\\) with a fresh integer \\(r_s\\neq1\\) and distinct from all previously used \\(r\\)’s.\n\nAfter the \\(t\\) steps we have:\n\n* **Sunny lines:** \\(\\ell_{i_1,j_1},\\dots,\\ell_{i_t,j_t}\\) – exactly \\(t\\) of them;\n* **Horizontal lines:** the original horizontals \\(y=j\\) for those \\(j\\) that were never selected; their number is \\((n-1)-t\\);\n* **Vertical lines:** the original line \\(x=1\\) (still present) together with the \\(t\\) newly added verticals \\(x=r_s\\); the total number of vertical lines is therefore \\(1\\).\n\nConsequently the total number of lines equals  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1-t)}_{\\text{horizontals}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{1}_{\\text{verticals}}\n\\;+\\;\n\\underbrace{t}_{\\text{sunny}} \\;=\\; n,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(t\\).  \nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-1\\), we obtain configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\) in that range.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nBegin with the configuration obtained for \\(t=n-1\\) in the previous paragraph.  \nIt consists of\n\n* the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i,i}\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) (obtained by repeatedly replacing the pair \\((i,i)\\));\n* the single vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\nThe original covering \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) also contained the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nd:\\; x+y=n+1,\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhich has not been removed yet.  \nThe diagonal \\(d\\) contains the points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n\\).  \nAll points \\((i,i)\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) are already covered by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i,i}\\).  \nThe two remaining diagonal points are \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\).\n\nReplace the diagonal \\(d\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; \\text{through }(1,n)\\text{ and }(2,1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-n}{2-1}=1-n,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(n\\ge3\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \nThe point \\((2,1)\\) already lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{2,1}\\), so after deleting \\(d\\) and adding \\(\\ell\\) every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered.\n\nNow the family consists of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i,i}\\) together with the new sunny line \\(\\ell\\); all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nA configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so it cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nNo other value of \\(k\\) is possible.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are attainable, and no others.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
