\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) are horizontal or vertical, hence none is sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) contains the points \\((a,j)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\); these are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(j\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on some line of \\(\\mathcal F_0\\).  \n\\(\\mathcal F_0\\) consists of \\((n-1)+1=n\\) distinct lines, so a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A local operation that creates a sunny line\n\nTake any \\((i,j)\\in T_n\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n-1\\).  \nConsider the two lines \\(x=i\\) and \\(y=j\\) that belong to the current family.  \nDelete these two lines and insert the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{i,j}:\\;y-j=m\\,(x-i),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope \\(m\\) is different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) and the line is not vertical, \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\) is sunny.  \nThe only point that could have been lost by removing \\(x=i\\) and \\(y=j\\) is the intersection \\((i,j)\\); this point now lies on \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\).  \nAll other points of \\(T_n\\) remain on the unchanged lines, so the covering property is preserved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Restoring the total number of lines\n\nThe operation (3) removes two lines and adds one sunny line, decreasing the total number of lines by one.  \nTo keep the total at \\(n\\) we add a new vertical line \\(x=r\\) where the integer \\(r\\) has not been used before.  \nA vertical line is never sunny, therefore the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one while the total number of lines stays \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(t\\) be any integer satisfying \\(0\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \nStarting from \\(\\mathcal F_0\\) we repeat the following procedure \\(t\\) times:\n\n1. Choose a pair \\((i_s,j_s)\\) such that the lines \\(x=i_s\\) and \\(y=j_s\\) are still present.\n2. Apply the replacement (3) to obtain the sunny line \\(\\ell_{i_s,j_s}\\).\n3. Add a new vertical line \\(x=r_s\\) with a fresh integer \\(r_s\\neq1\\) and distinct from all previously introduced \\(r\\)\u2019s.\n\nAfter the \\(t\\) repetitions we have  \n\n* **Sunny lines:** \\(\\ell_{i_1,j_1},\\dots ,\\ell_{i_t,j_t}\\) \u2013 exactly \\(t\\) of them;\n* **Horizontal lines:** those \\(y=j\\) for which \\(j\\) has never been selected; there are \\((n-1)-t\\) of them;\n* **Vertical lines:** the original line \\(x=1\\) together with the \\(t\\) newly added verticals \\(x=r_s\\); altogether \\(1+t\\) vertical lines.\n\nHence the total number of lines equals  \n\n\\[\n(n-1-t)+(1+t)+t=n,\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nand the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(t\\).  \nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-1\\), every \\(k\\) in that range is realized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Impossibility of having \\(n\\) sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\nConsider the first column of points \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset T_n\\).  \nA sunny line can contain at most one point of this column, because two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate would force the line to be vertical, which is not sunny.  \nTherefore we need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines to cover the \\(n\\) points of the first column, and each of those lines can cover **only one** of them.\n\nNow look at the second column \\(\\{(2,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\\).  \nThe line that already covers \\((1,1)\\) cannot be used for any point of the second column (different \\(x\\)-coordinate).  \nConsequently we need a *different* line for each of the \\(n-1\\) points of the second column.  \nTogether with the line already used for \\((1,1)\\) this already requires \\(n\\) distinct lines, leaving **no line** left to cover the remaining columns \\(x=3,4,\\dots ,n\\).  \nThus the assumption that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny leads to a contradiction.\n\nHence a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nWe have also proved that a family of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,}.\n\\]"

