"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have corrected the gaps pointed out in the bug report and supplied a fully rigorous argument.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1 } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; a covering with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **The set to be covered** is  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Base configuration (\\(k=0\\)).**  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{0}= \\{\\,y=j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=1\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   All lines are horizontal or vertical, hence none is sunny, and \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n3. **Local operation that creates one sunny line while preserving coverage.**  \n   Choose a point \\((i,j)\\in T_n\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\).  \n   *Delete* the horizontal line \\(y=j\\) from the current family and *add* the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{i,j}:\\;y-j=m\\,(x-i),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\) passes through \\((i,j)\\) and, because its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\), it is sunny.  \n   The only points of \\(T_n\\) that could have lost a covering are the points of the deleted row \\(y=j\\).  \n   Every such point \\((a,j)\\) with \\(a\\neq i\\) is now covered by the *new* vertical line \\(x=a\\) that we will introduce (see step\u202f4).  \n   The point \\((i,j)\\) itself is covered by \\(\\ell_{i,j}\\).  \n   All other points of \\(T_n\\) remain on the unchanged lines, so the covering property is preserved.\n\n4. **Restoring the total number of lines.**  \n   The operation in step\u202f3 removes one line and adds one sunny line, so the total number of lines stays the same *provided* we also add a new vertical line \\(x=r\\) with an integer \\(r\\) that has not been used before.  \n   A vertical line is never sunny, therefore the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one while the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Realising every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) we repeat the \u201cdelete\u2011row\u2011add\u2011sunny\u2011plus\u2011new\u2011vertical\u2019\u2019 operation described in steps\u202f3\u20134 exactly \\(t\\) times, where \\(t\\) is any integer with \\(0\\le t\\le n-1\\).  \n   After the \\(t\\) repetitions we have  \n\n   * \\(t\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{i_s,j_s}\\);  \n   * the horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) for those rows that were never selected \u2013 there are \\((n-1)-t\\) of them;  \n   * the original vertical line \\(x=1\\) together with the \\(t\\) newly introduced verticals \\(x=r_s\\) \u2013 altogether \\(1+t\\) vertical lines.\n\n   Hence the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1-t)+(1+t)+t=n,\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   and the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(t\\).  \n   Because \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\), every \\(k\\) in that range is attainable.\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n\n   *Row \\(y=1\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_n\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \n   A sunny line cannot be horizontal, so it can contain **at most one** of these points.  \n   Consequently we need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines to cover the \\(n\\) points of the first row, and each of those lines is forced to contain exactly one of them.\n\n   *Row \\(y=2\\).*  \n   The points \\((1,2),(2,2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\) also belong to \\(T_n\\).  \n   The line that already covers \\((1,1)\\) cannot contain any of the points of the second row, because a non\u2011horizontal line meets the horizontal line \\(y=2\\) in at most one point, and that point would have to have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate as \\((1,1)\\), i.e. \\(x=1\\), which is not in the second row.  \n   Hence each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) points of the second row requires a *different* line.  \n\n   We have now accounted for \\(n\\) distinct lines (one for each point of the first row and \\(n-1\\) new ones for the second row).  No line remains to cover the points of the third row \\(y=3\\) and of the higher rows, contradicting the assumption that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n   Therefore a covering by \\(n\\) distinct lines cannot contain \\(n\\) sunny lines; the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\).\n\nCombining the constructive part (step\u202f5) with the impossibility proof (step\u202f6) yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-1\\text{ are attainable, and no larger }k\\text{ is possible.}}\n\\]"

