            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), and \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound: at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines\n\nConsider the leftmost column of \\(T_n\\), namely the set  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is neither vertical nor horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\); therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(C_1\\). Consequently, to cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) we need at least one line that is **vertical** (i.e. non‑sunny). Hence any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) must contain at least one non‑sunny line, and therefore can contain at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n#### 2.1 Base family (no sunny lines)\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_n=\\{\\,x=i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\,\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny. Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so it lies on the line \\(x=a\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.2 Sunny line that replaces a vertical line\n\nFor an integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\; y = (c-1)-(c-2)\\,x .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Slope.* The slope of \\(\\ell_c\\) equals \\(-(c-2)\\). Because \\(c\\ge 3\\), we have \\(-(c-2)\\neq 0\\), \\(-(c-2)\\neq -1\\), and the line is not vertical; hence \\(\\ell_c\\) is sunny.\n\n*Coverage of column \\(x=c\\).* Substituting \\(x=c\\) into (6) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = (c-1)-(c-2)c = 1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((c,1)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_c\\). Moreover, the equation of \\(\\ell_c\\) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = -(c-2)(x-c) .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((c,b)\\) is any point of the column \\(x=c\\) that lies in \\(T_n\\) (so \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\)), then \\(b-1\\) is a multiple of \\(-(c-2)\\) because  \n\n\\[\nb-1 = -(c-2)\\cdot 0 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((c,b)\\) satisfies (7) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_c\\). Consequently **every** point of the column \\(x=c\\) belonging to \\(T_n\\) is on \\(\\ell_c\\).\n\n*Intersection with the preceding column.* Setting \\(x=c-1\\) in (6) yields  \n\n\\[\ny = (c-1)-(c-2)(c-1)=c-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((c-1,c-1)\\) (which belongs to the column \\(x=c-1\\)) also lies on \\(\\ell_c\\).\n\n#### 2.3 Replacement procedure\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-1\\). Starting from the base family \\(\\mathcal V_n\\) we perform the following operation for the columns  \n\n\\[\nc=n,\\;n-1,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-t+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(c\\) we **delete** the vertical line \\(x=c\\) and **add** the sunny line \\(\\ell_c\\) defined in (6).\n\nBecause of the properties proved in §2.2:\n\n* after deleting \\(x=c\\) the column \\(x=c\\) remains covered (by \\(\\ell_c\\));\n* the point \\((c-1,c-1)\\) of the next column is now covered by \\(\\ell_c\\).  \n  When the next step deletes the vertical line \\(x=c-1\\), the whole column \\(x=c-1\\) will already be covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{c-1}\\), and the point \\((c-2,c-2)\\) will be covered by \\(\\ell_{c-1}\\), and so on.\n\nThus no point of \\(T_n\\) is ever uncovered during the whole process.\n\nAfter the \\(t\\) replacements the resulting family, denoted \\(\\mathcal F_t\\), consists of\n\n* the sunny lines \\(\\ell_n,\\ell_{n-1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n-t+1}\\) (exactly \\(t\\) of them);\n* the remaining vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-t\\) (exactly \\(n-t\\) of them).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal F_t| = t+(n-t)=n\\) and the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) is precisely \\(t\\).\n\nBecause the construction works for every integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-1\\), we have exhibited, for each such \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Final answer\n\nThe maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\), and every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\) can be realized.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n-1\\text{ are attainable, and no larger }k\\text{ is possible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
